{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09498","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09498","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09498","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yinzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiantao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jiantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingying","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yingying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhimin","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zhimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"You","name":"You, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lynn","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Lynn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lewei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Lewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SEDM: Selective Self-Evolving Distributed Memory for Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T14:37:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:39:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T13:52:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:55:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T06:26:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:35:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T06:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-15T00:44:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agents in a society can reuse each other's experience, but indiscriminate memory use increases processing cost and can reduce task success. We present SEDM (Selective Self-Evolving Distributed Memory), a framework that selects both what agents share and when recipients use it. On the sender side, SEDM admits memories through paired execution tests, ranks them by relevance and measured utility, and abstracts them for cross-domain reuse. On the recipient side, Calibrated Memory Gating (CMG) predicts success with and without memory in a single forward pass, learning from paired outcomes including ties. It injects memory only when the calibrated success gain outweighs a context-cost penalty. SEDM exceeds the strongest evaluated baseline on LoCoMo open-domain questions by 15.1 F1 points and outperforms G-Memory on FEVER and HotpotQA with 16-32% fewer prompt tokens. In a controlled HotpotQA study, CMG achieves success comparable to always using memory with 18% fewer agent input tokens. Within the full SEDM pipeline on LoCoMo, CMG outperforms both always-use and no-memory policies for two SEDM memories while using 19-31% fewer input tokens than always-use. These results show that selective use can recover value from shared memories that hurt performance when injected indiscriminately."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09498","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:35:23Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:35:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-15T03:09:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09215","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09215","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09215","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinnan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qinnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linkang","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Linkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qichao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enabling Regulatory Multi-Agent Collaboration: Architecture, Challenges, and Solutions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:46:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:20:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-20T11:46:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-21T00:54:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-28T15:11:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-29T03:10:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs)-empowered autonomous agents are transforming both digital and physical environments by enabling adaptive, multi-agent collaboration. While these agents offer significant opportunities across domains such as finance, healthcare, and smart manufacturing, their unpredictable behaviors and heterogeneous capabilities pose substantial governance and accountability challenges. In this paper, we propose a blockchain-enabled layered architecture for regulatory agent collaboration, comprising an agent layer, an off-chain computation layer, and an on-chain anchoring layer. Within this framework, we design three key modules: (i) an agent behavior tracing and arbitration module for automated accountability, (ii) a dynamic reputation evaluation module for trust assessment in collaborative scenarios, and (iii) a malicious behavior forecasting module for early detection of adversarial activities. Our approach establishes a systematic foundation for trustworthy, resilient, and scalable regulatory mechanisms in large-scale agent ecosystems. Finally, we discuss the future research directions for blockchain-enabled regulatory frameworks in multi-agent systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09215","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:40Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08897","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08897","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08897","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Caffagni","name":"Caffagni, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Sarto","name":"Sarto, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcella","familyName":"Cornia","name":"Cornia, Marcella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Baraldi","name":"Baraldi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rita","familyName":"Cucchiara","name":"Cucchiara, Rita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Recurrence Meets Transformers for Universal Multimodal Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:00:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:01:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T08:11:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:32:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T12:08:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:49:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of multimodal retrieval and its application in LLMs and multimodal LLMs, increasingly complex retrieval tasks have emerged. Existing methods predominantly rely on task-specific fine-tuning of vision-language models and are limited to single-modality queries or documents. In this paper, we propose ReT-2, a unified retrieval model that supports multimodal queries, composed of both images and text, and searches across multimodal document collections where text and images coexist. ReT-2 leverages multi-layer representations and a recurrent Transformer architecture with LSTM-inspired gating mechanisms to dynamically integrate information across layers and modalities, capturing fine-grained visual and textual details. We evaluate ReT-2 on the challenging M2KR and M-BEIR benchmarks across different retrieval configurations. Results demonstrate that ReT-2 consistently achieves state-of-the-art performance across diverse settings, while offering faster inference and reduced memory usage compared to prior approaches. When integrated into retrieval-augmented generation pipelines, ReT-2 also improves downstream performance on Encyclopedic-VQA and InfoSeek datasets. Our source code and trained models are publicly available at: https://github.com/aimagelab/ReT-2"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"TPAMI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08897","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:21:10Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:21:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:40:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08832","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08832","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08832","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasily","familyName":"Melnikov","name":"Melnikov, Vasily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal Risk Sharing Without Preference Convexity: An Aggregate Convexity Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Finance (q-fin.MF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Risk Management (q-fin.RM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"91B05, 91G70","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-26T19:43:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T17:45:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-26T01:16:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider the optimal risk sharing problem with a continuum of agents, modeled via a non-atomic measure space. Individual preferences are not assumed to be convex. We show the multiplicity of agents induces the value function to be convex, allowing for the application of convex duality techniques to risk sharing without preference convexity. A computationally tractable formula for the conjugate of the value function is derived, yielding an explicit dual representation of the value function. Applications of our results include a version of the two fundamental theorems of welfare economics for a large class of non-convex preferences, and non-existence results for Pareto optima when preferences are distortion risk measures whose corresponding distortion function fails to majorize the identity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08832","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:19:38Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:19:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:36:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09544","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09544","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09544","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paolo","familyName":"Pedinotti","name":"Pedinotti, Paolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Baumann","name":"Baumann, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan","familyName":"Jessurun","name":"Jessurun, Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leslie","familyName":"Barrett","name":"Barrett, Leslie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrico","familyName":"Santus","name":"Santus, Enrico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaGraph: A Large-Scale Meta-Analysis of GenAI in Financial NLP (2022-2025)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:37:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:42:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-12T21:35:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-14T00:14:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T16:53:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-27T01:15:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Financial NLP has evolved rapidly since late 2022, outpacing narrative surveys. We introduce MetaGraph, a methodology for extracting typed knowledge graphs from scientific corpora using ontology-guided LLM extraction to enable structured, large-scale trend analysis. Applied to 681 papers on GenAI in Finance (2022-2025), MetaGraph reveals three phases: early LLM-driven expansion of tasks and datasets, growing emphasis on limitations and risk, and a shift toward modular, system-oriented methods (e.g., retrieval-augmented designs). We release the resulting resource and artifacts to support reproducible meta-analysis and future monitoring of the field."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, appendices, GEM, ACL"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09544","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:28Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:35:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09505","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09505","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09505","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binglei","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Binglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey T. H.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Jeffrey T. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwen","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Zhiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Przemyslaw","familyName":"Forys","name":"Forys, Przemyslaw","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyang","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Chengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timi","familyName":"Adeniran","name":"Adeniran, Timi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wayne","familyName":"Luk","name":"Luk, Wayne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxiang","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Hongxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyi","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy M.","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Timothy M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rika","familyName":"Antonova","name":"Antonova, Rika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Mullins","name":"Mullins, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Combating the Memory Walls: Optimization Pathways for Long-Context Agentic LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T14:49:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:40:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T11:31:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:43:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-12T10:29:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:00:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs now form the backbone of AI agents across a diverse range of applications, including tool use, command-line interfaces, and web or computer interaction. These agentic LLM inference tasks are fundamentally different from chatbot-focused inference. They often involve much longer context lengths to capture complex and prolonged inputs, such as an entire webpage DOM or complicated tool-call trajectories. This, in turn, generates significant off-chip memory traffic during inference and causes workloads to be constrained by two memory walls, namely the bandwidth wall and the capacity wall, preventing compute units from achieving high utilization.\n In this paper, we introduce PLENA, a hardware-software co-designed system built around three core optimization pathways. PLENA features a novel flattened systolic-array architecture (Pathway 1) and efficient compute and memory units that support an asymmetric quantization scheme (Pathway 2). It also provides native support for FlashAttention (Pathway 3). In addition, PLENA includes a complete software-hardware stack, consisting of a custom ISA, a compiler, a transaction-level simulator, and an automated design-space exploration flow. Experimental results show that PLENA delivers up to 2.23x and 4.70x higher throughput than the A100 GPU and TPU v6e, respectively, under identical multiplier counts and memory configurations during LLaMA agentic inference. PLENA also achieves up to 4.04x higher energy efficiency than the A100 GPU. The full PLENA system, including its simulator, compiler, ISA, and RTL implementation, will be open-sourced to the research community."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09505","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:35:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:35:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:17:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09438","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09438","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09438","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziquan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ioannis","familyName":"Patras","name":"Patras, Ioannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GrACE: A Generative Approach to Better Confidence Elicitation and Efficient Test-Time Scaling in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:25:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:36:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-07T04:24:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-08T00:26:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Assessing the reliability of Large Language Models (LLMs) by confidence elicitation is a prominent approach to AI safety in high-stakes applications, such as healthcare and finance. Existing methods either require expensive computational overhead or suffer from poor calibration, making them impractical and unreliable for real-world deployment. In this work, we propose GrACE, a Generative Approach to Confidence Elicitation that enables scalable and reliable confidence elicitation for LLMs. GrACE adopts a novel mechanism in which the model expresses confidence by the similarity between the last hidden state and the embedding of a special token appended to the vocabulary, in real-time. We fine-tune the model for calibrating the confidence with targets associated with accuracy. Extensive experiments show that the confidence produced by GrACE achieves the best discriminative capacity and calibration on open-ended generation tasks without resorting to additional sampling or an auxiliary model. Moreover, we propose two confidence-based strategies for test-time scaling with GrACE, which not only improve the accuracy of the final decision but also significantly reduce the number of required samples, highlighting its potential as a practical solution for deploying LLMs with reliable, on-the-fly confidence estimation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09438","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:33:59Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:33:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-08T02:30:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09525","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09525","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09525","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialiang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jialiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Teng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sixing","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Sixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinlei","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jinlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingdi","familyName":"Shan","name":"Shan, Yingdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongwei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengting","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Mengting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haifeng","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Haifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Mingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TrEnv-X: Transparently Share Serverless Execution Environments Across Different Functions and Nodes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Operating Systems (cs.OS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:06:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:41:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-21T12:02:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T00:38:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Serverless computing is renowned for its computation elasticity, yet its full potential is often constrained by the requirement for functions to operate within local and dedicated background environments, resulting in limited memory elasticity. To address this limitation, this paper introduces TrEnv-X, a co-designed integration of the serverless platform with the operating system and CXL/RDMA-based remote memory pools. TrEnv-X's core innovations are repurposable sandboxes, which can be shared across different functions to decrease the associated creation overhead, and OS-level memory templates, which enable rapid state restoration from CXL/RDMA-based remote memory pools. To further demonstrate TrEnv-X's versatility, we generalize its design from traditional containers for microVM-based agent workloads and introduce new optimizations, including browser sharing and a page cache bypassing mechanism. Our evaluation shows that TrEnv-X achieves up to 7x reduction in P99 latency and 48% memory savings for container-based functions. When applied to LLM agents, it reduces the P99 latency by up to 58% and memory usage by 61% compared to state-of-the-art systems like E2B."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM Transactions on Computer Systems (TOCS)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09525","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:01Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:15:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09135","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09135","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09135","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuefeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xuefeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henglin","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Henglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed H.","familyName":"Qureshi","name":"Qureshi, Ahmed H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Husheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Husheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Continuous-Time Value Iteration for Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T04:12:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:14:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T23:07:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:10:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-19T04:56:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-20T01:23:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing reinforcement learning (RL) methods struggle with complex dynamical systems that demand interactions at high frequencies or irregular time intervals. Continuous-time RL (CTRL) has emerged as a promising alternative by replacing discrete-time Bellman recursion with differential value functions defined as viscosity solutions of the Hamilton--Jacobi--Bellman (HJB) equation. While CTRL has shown promise, its applications have been largely limited to the single-agent domain. This limitation stems from two key challenges: (i) conventional solution methods for HJB equations suffer from the curse of dimensionality (CoD), making them intractable in high-dimensional systems; and (ii) even with HJB-based learning approaches, accurately approximating centralized value functions in multi-agent settings remains difficult, which in turn destabilizes policy training. In this paper, we propose a CT-MARL framework that uses physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) to approximate HJB-based value functions at scale. To ensure the value is consistent with its differential structure, we align value learning with value-gradient learning by introducing a Value Gradient Iteration (VGI) module that iteratively refines value gradients along trajectories. This improves gradient fidelity, in turn yielding more accurate values and stronger policy learning. We evaluate our method using continuous-time variants of standard benchmarks, including multi-agent particle environment (MPE) and multi-agent MuJoCo. Our results demonstrate that our approach consistently outperforms existing continuous-time RL baselines and scales to complex multi-agent dynamics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026. 21 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09135","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:44Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-20T03:02:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09660","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09660","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09660","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Fayyaz","name":"Fayyaz, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Modarressi","name":"Modarressi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanieh","familyName":"Deilamsalehy","name":"Deilamsalehy, Hanieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franck","familyName":"Dernoncourt","name":"Dernoncourt, Franck","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trung","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Trung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hinrich","familyName":"Schütze","name":"Schütze, Hinrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Nanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Steering MoE LLMs via Expert (De)Activation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:55:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:49:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T07:34:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:33:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) in Large Language Models (LLMs) routes each token through a subset of specialized Feed-Forward Networks (FFN), known as experts. We present SteerMoE, a framework to steer MoE models by detecting and controlling behavior-associated experts. We detect key experts by comparing how often they activate between paired inputs that demonstrate opposite behaviors (e.g., safe vs. unsafe). By selectively activating or deactivating such experts during inference, we control behaviors like faithfulness and safety without fine-tuning. Across 11 benchmarks and 6 LLMs, our steering raises safety by up to +20% and faithfulness by +27%. Alternatively, unsafe steering drops safety by -41% alone, and -100% when combined with existing jailbreak methods, bypassing all safety guardrails. Overall, SteerMoE offers a lightweight, effective, and widely applicable test-time control, while revealing unique vulnerabilities in MoE LLMs. https://github.com/adobe-research/SteerMoE"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09660","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:39:10Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:39:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:20:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09679","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09679","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09679","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Bingxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oussama","familyName":"Elachqar","name":"Elachqar, Oussama","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhang","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Yuzhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ButterflyQuant: Ultra-low-bit LLM Quantization through Learnable Orthogonal Butterfly Transforms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:59:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:49:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T15:12:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:57:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T23:12:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-12T01:12:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models require massive memory footprints, severely limiting deployment on consumer hardware. Quantization reduces memory through lower numerical precision, but extreme 2-bit quantization suffers from catastrophic performance loss due to outliers in activations. Rotation-based methods such as QuIP and QuaRot apply orthogonal transforms to eliminate outliers before quantization, using computational invariance: $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{Wx} = (\\mathbf{WQ}^T)(\\mathbf{Qx})$ for orthogonal $\\mathbf{Q}$. However, these methods use fixed transforms--Hadamard matrices achieving optimal worst-case coherence $μ= 1/\\sqrt{n}$--that cannot adapt to specific weight distributions. We identify that different transformer layers exhibit distinct outlier patterns, motivating layer-adaptive rotations rather than one-size-fits-all approaches. In this work, we propose ButterflyQuant, which replaces Hadamard rotations with learnable butterfly transforms parameterized by continuous Givens rotation angles. Unlike Hadamard's discrete $\\{+1, -1\\}$ entries that are non-differentiable and thus prohibit gradient-based learning, butterfly transforms' continuous parameterization enables smooth optimization while guaranteeing orthogonality by construction. This orthogonal constraint ensures theoretical guarantees in outlier suppression while achieving $O(n \\log n)$ computational complexity with only $\\frac{n \\log n}{2}$ learnable parameters. We further introduce a uniformity regularization on post-transformation activations to promote smoother distributions amenable to quantization. Learning requires only 128 calibration samples and converges in minutes on a single GPU."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Replace discrete Hadamard transforms with continuous Butterfly transforms to facilitate the learning of rotation matrices in LLM quantization"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09679","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:39:37Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:39:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-12T03:20:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09170","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09170","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09170","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Davies","name":"Davies, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirudh","familyName":"Sankar","name":"Sankar, Anirudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The value of conceptual knowledge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T06:11:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:18:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T23:03:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-12T01:11:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the instrumental value of conceptual knowledge when making statistical decisions. Such knowledge tells agents how unknown, payoff-relevant states relate. It is distinct from the statistical knowledge gained from observing signals of those states. We formalize this distinction in a tractable framework used by economists and statisticians. Conceptual knowledge is valuable because it empowers agents to design more informative signals. It is more valuable when states are more \"reducible\": when they can be explained with fewer common concepts. Its value is non-monotone in the number of signals and vanishes when agents have infinitely many signals. Agents who know more concepts can attain the same payoffs with fewer signals. This is especially true when states are highly reducible."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"66 pages, 5 figures. v2 replaces \"features\" with \"concepts\", tightens exposition, and expands on related literature"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09170","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:35Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-12T03:20:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09199","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09199","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09199","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bangcheng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Bangcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daohai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Daohai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Chao","name":"Chao, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongrong","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Rongrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CCF: A Context Compression Framework for Efficient Long-Sequence Language Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:13:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:19:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-02T13:56:03Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; The quality of this paper is low"},{"date":"2026-02-03T03:02:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling language models to longer contexts is essential for capturing rich dependencies across extended discourse. However, naïve context extension imposes significant computational and memory burdens, often resulting in inefficiencies during both training and inference. In this work, we propose CCF, a novel context compression framework designed to enable efficient long-context modeling by learning hierarchical latent representations that preserve global semantics while aggressively reducing input redundancy. CCF integrates segment-wise semantic aggregation with key-value memory encoding, forming compact representations that support accurate reconstruction and long-range understanding. To further enhance scalability, we introduce a training-efficient optimization strategy that couples incremental segment decoding with sparse reservoir sampling, substantially reducing memory overhead without degrading performance. Empirical results on multiple long-context language modeling benchmarks demonstrate that CCF achieves competitive perplexity under high compression ratios, and significantly improves throughput and memory efficiency compared to existing approaches. These findings highlight the potential of structured compression for scalable and effective long-context language modeling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The quality of this paper is low"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09199","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:17Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:38:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09332","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09332","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09332","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuecheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dafeng","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Dafeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiguang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanguang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhanguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzheng","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Yuzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengwei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Pengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Gamaliel Arcos","familyName":"Bravo","name":"Bravo, David Gamaliel Arcos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingxue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yingxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianye","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Jianye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyue","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Xingyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniEVA: Embodied Versatile Planner via Task-Adaptive 3D-Grounded and Embodiment-aware Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:32:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:28:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T08:01:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:22:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T05:17:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:23:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have opened new opportunities for embodied intelligence, enabling multimodal understanding, reasoning, and interaction, as well as continuous spatial decision-making. Nevertheless, current MLLM-based embodied systems face two critical limitations. First, Geometric Adaptability Gap: models trained solely on 2D inputs or with hard-coded 3D geometry injection suffer from either insufficient spatial information or restricted 2D generalization, leading to poor adaptability across tasks with diverse spatial demands. Second, Embodiment Constraint Gap: prior work often neglects the physical constraints and capacities of real robots, resulting in task plans that are theoretically valid but practically infeasible. To address these gaps, we introduce OmniEVA -- an embodied versatile planner that enables advanced embodied reasoning and task planning through two pivotal innovations: (1) a Task-Adaptive 3D Grounding mechanism, which introduces a gated router to perform explicit selective regulation of 3D fusion based on contextual requirements, enabling context-aware 3D grounding for diverse embodied tasks. (2) an Embodiment-Aware Reasoning framework that jointly incorporates task goals and embodiment constraints into the reasoning loop, resulting in planning decisions that are both goal-directed and executable. Extensive experimental results demonstrate that OmniEVA not only achieves state-of-the-art general embodied reasoning performance, but also exhibits a strong ability across a wide range of downstream scenarios. Evaluations of a suite of proposed embodied benchmarks, including both primitive and composite tasks, confirm its robust and versatile planning capabilities. Project page: https://omnieva.github.io"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09332","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:31:28Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-29T03:12:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Crystal","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Crystal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kehang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Kehang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Horton","name":"Horton, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin S.","familyName":"Manning","name":"Manning, Benjamin S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vivian","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Vivian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Wexler","name":"Wexler, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nithum","familyName":"Thain","name":"Thain, Nithum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Strategic Tradeoffs Between Humans and AI in Multi-Agent Bargaining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T00:25:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:09:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T18:52:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:05:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T22:19:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:11:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-24T01:24:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:12:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Markets increasingly accommodate large language models (LLMs) as autonomous decision-making agents. As this transition occurs, it becomes critical to evaluate how these agents behave relative to their human and task-specific statistical predecessors. In this work, we present results from an empirical study comparing humans (N=216), multiple frontier LLMs, and customized Bayesian agents in dynamic multi-player bargaining games under identical conditions. Bayesian agents extract the highest surplus with aggressive trade proposals that are frequently rejected. Humans and LLMs achieve comparable aggregate surplus within their groups, but exhibit different trading strategies. LLMs favor conservative, concessionary proposals that are usually accepted by other LLMs, while humans propose trades that are consistent with fairness norms but are more likely to be rejected. These findings highlight that performance parity -- a common benchmark in agent evaluation -- can mask substantive procedural differences in how LLMs behave in complex multi-agent interactions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:11Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:34:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09219","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09219","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09219","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakob","familyName":"Nyberg","name":"Nyberg, Jakob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pontus","familyName":"Johnson","name":"Johnson, Pontus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vejde: A Framework for Inductive Deep Reinforcement Learning Based on Factor Graph Color Refinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:51:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:21:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-19T14:07:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:27:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present and evaluate Vejde; a framework which combines data abstraction, graph neural networks and reinforcement learning to produce inductive policy functions for decision problems with richly structured states, such as object classes and relations. MDP states are represented as data bases of facts about entities, and Vejde converts each state to a bipartite graph, which is mapped to latent states through neural message passing. The factored representation of both states and actions allows Vejde agents to handle problems of varying size and structure. We tested Vejde agents on eight problem domains defined in RDDL, with ten problem instances each, where policies were trained using both supervised and reinforcement learning. To test policy generalization, we separate problem instances in two sets, one for training and the other solely for testing. Test results on unseen instances for the Vejde agents were compared to MLP agents trained on each problem instance, as well as the online planning algorithm Prost. Our results show that Vejde policies in average generalize to the test instances without a significant loss in score. Additionally, the inductive agents received scores on unseen test instances that on average were close to the instance-specific MLP agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09219","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:46Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:04:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09583","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09583","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09583","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brittany","familyName":"Harbison","name":"Harbison, Brittany","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Taubman","name":"Taubman, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Travis","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Travis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashok. K.","familyName":"Goel","name":"Goel, Ashok. K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Personality-Enhanced Social Recommendations in SAMI: Exploring the Role of Personality Detection in Matchmaking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:19:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:45:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-06T08:54:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:31:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Social belonging is a vital part of learning, yet online course environments present barriers to the organic formation of social groups. SAMI (Social Agent Mediated Interactions) offers one solution by facilitating student connections, but its effectiveness may be constrained by an incomplete Theory of Mind, limiting its ability to create an effective 'mental model' of a student. One facet of this is its inability to intuit personality, which may influence the relevance of its recommendations.\n To explore this gap, we examine the viability of automated personality inference by proposing a personality detection model utilizing GPT's zeroshot capability to infer Big-Five personality traits from forum introduction posts, often encouraged in online courses. We benchmark its performance against established models, finding that while GPT models show promising results on this specific dataset, performance varies significantly across traits. We identify potential biases toward optimistic trait inference, particularly for traits with skewed distributions.\n We demonstrate a proof-of-concept integration of personality detection into SAMI's entity-based matchmaking system, focusing on three traits with established connections to positive social formation: Extroversion, Agreeableness, and Openness. This work represents an initial exploration of personality-informed social recommendations in educational settings. While our implementation shows technical feasibility, significant questions remain. We discuss these limitations and outline directions for future work, examining what LLMs specifically capture when performing personality inference and whether personality-based matching meaningfully improves student connections in practice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. Appears in INTED 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09583","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:37:22Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:37:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-07T02:59:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09161","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09161","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09161","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chayan","familyName":"Chatterjee","name":"Chatterjee, Chayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaylah","familyName":"McGowan","name":"McGowan, Kaylah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suyash","familyName":"Deshmukh","name":"Deshmukh, Suyash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karan","familyName":"Jani","name":"Jani, Karan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Machine Learning Confirms GW231123 is a \"Lite\" Intermediate Mass Black Hole Merger"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T05:42:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:17:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:33:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:16:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3847/2041-8213/ae1a5f","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA Collaboration recently reported GW231123, a black hole merger with total mass of around 190-265 solar mass. This event adds to the growing evidence of \"lite\" intermediate mass black hole (IMBH) discoveries of post-merger black holes \u0026gt;100 solar mass. GW231123 posed several data analysis challenges owing to waveform-model systematics and presence of noise artifacts called glitches. We present the first comprehensive machine learning analysis to further validate this event, strengthen its astrophysical inference, and characterize instrumental noise in its vicinity. Our approach uses a combination of tools tailored for specific analyses: GW-Whisper, an adaptation of OpenAI's audio transformer, ArchGEM, a Gaussian mixture model-based soft clustering and density approximation software and AWaRe, a convolutional autoencoder. We identify the data segment containing the merger with \u0026gt;70% confidence in both detectors and verify its astrophysical origin. We then characterize the scattered light glitch around the event, providing the first physically interpretable parameters for the glitch. We also reconstruct the real waveforms from the data with slightly better agreement to model-agnostic reconstructions than to quasi-circular models, hinting at possible astrophysics beyond current waveform families (such as non-circular orbits or environmental imprints). Finally, by demonstrating high-fidelity waveform reconstructions for simulated mergers with total masses between 100-1000 solar mass, we show that our method can confidently probe the IMBH regime. Our integrated framework offers a powerful complementary tool to traditional pipelines for rapid, robust analysis of massive, glitch-contaminated events."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09161","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:23Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-08T02:46:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09245","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09245","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09245","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuocheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silin","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Silin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeye","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yeye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongmei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dongmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Jupiter: Enhancing LLM Data Analysis Capabilities via Notebook and Inference-Time Value-Guided Search"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:27:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:22:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-03T09:56:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:36:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown great promise in automating data science workflows, but existing models still struggle with multi-step reasoning and tool use, which limits their effectiveness on complex data analysis tasks. To address this, we propose a scalable pipeline that extracts high-quality, tool-based data analysis tasks and their executable multi-step solutions from real-world Jupyter notebooks and associated data files. Using this pipeline, we introduce NbQA, a large-scale dataset of standardized task-solution pairs that reflect authentic tool-use patterns in practical data science scenarios. To further enhance multi-step reasoning, we present Jupiter, a framework that formulates data analysis as a search problem and applies Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) to generate diverse solution trajectories for value model learning. During inference, Jupiter combines the value model and node visit counts to efficiently collect executable multi-step plans with minimal search steps. Experimental results show that Qwen2.5-7B and 14B-Instruct models on NbQA solve 77.82% and 86.38% of tasks on InfiAgent-DABench, respectively-matching or surpassing GPT-4o and advanced agent frameworks. Further evaluations demonstrate improved generalization and stronger tool-use reasoning across diverse multi-step reasoning tasks. Code and data are available at https://github.com/microsoft/Jupiter."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AAAI 2026 (Main Technical Track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09245","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:23Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-04T18:40:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08863","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08863","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08863","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianqian","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Qianqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingming","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Qingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liuchang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Liuchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sensen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Sensen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruichen","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Ruichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hailin","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Hailin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhong","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Zhenhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoJSON Agents:A Multi-Agent LLM Architecture for Geospatial Analysis-Function Calling vs Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T03:43:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T08:26:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:23:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-03T07:42:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:30:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated substantial progress in task automation and natural language understanding. However, without domain expertise in geographic information science (GIS), they continue to encounter limitations including reduced accuracy and unstable performance when processing complex tasks. To address these challenges, we propose GeoJSON Agents-a novel multi-agent LLM architecture specifically designed for geospatial analysis. This framework transforms natural language instructions into structured GeoJSON operations through two LLM enhancement techniques: Function Calling and Code Generation. The architecture integrates three core components: task parsing, agent collaboration, and result integration. The Planner agent systematically decomposes user-defined tasks into executable subtasks, while Worker agents perform spatial data processing and analysis either by invoking predefined function APIs or by generating and executing Python-based analytical code. The system produces reusable, standards-compliant GeoJSON outputs through iterative refinement. To evaluate both approaches, we constructed a benchmark comprising 70 tasks spanning basic, intermediate, and advanced complexity levels, conducting experiments with OpenAI's GPT-4o as the core model. Results indicate that the Code Generation-based agent achieved 97.14% accuracy, while the Function Calling-based agent attained 85.71%-both significantly outperforming the best-performing general-purpose model (48.57%). Comparative analysis reveals Code Generation offers superior flexibility for complex, open-ended tasks, whereas Function Calling provides enhanced execution stability for structured operations. This study represents the first systematic integration of GeoJSON data with a multi-agent LLM framework and provides empirical evidence comparing two mainstream enhancement methodologies in geospatial context."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08863","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:20:22Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:20:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-04T18:40:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09168","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09168","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09168","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omar","familyName":"Erak","name":"Erak, Omar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omar","familyName":"Alhussein","name":"Alhussein, Omar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hatem","familyName":"Abou-Zeid","name":"Abou-Zeid, Hatem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehdi","familyName":"Bennis","name":"Bennis, Mehdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Pareto-Optimal Token Merging for Edge Transformer Models in Semantic Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T06:05:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:17:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-14T14:05:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-17T01:46:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale transformer models have emerged as a powerful tool for semantic communication systems, enabling edge devices to extract rich representations for robust inference across noisy wireless channels. However, their substantial computational demands remain a major barrier to practical deployment in resource-constrained 6G networks. In this paper, we present a training-free framework for adaptive token merging in pretrained vision transformers to jointly reduce inference time and transmission resource usage. We formulate the selection of per-layer merging proportions as a multi-objective optimization problem to balance accuracy and computational cost. We employ Gaussian process-based Bayesian optimization to construct a Pareto frontier of optimal configurations, enabling flexible runtime adaptation to dynamic application requirements and channel conditions. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms other baselines and achieves significant reductions in floating-point operations while maintaining competitive accuracy across a wide range of signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) conditions. Additional results highlight the effectiveness of adaptive policies that adjust merging aggressiveness in response to channel quality, providing a practical mechanism to trade off latency and semantic fidelity on demand. These findings establish a scalable and efficient approach for deploying transformer-based semantic communication in future edge intelligence systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for presentation in IEEE Globecom 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09168","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-17T03:05:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09360","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09360","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09360","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Channdeth","familyName":"Sok","name":"Sok, Channdeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Luz","name":"Luz, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yacine","familyName":"Haddam","name":"Haddam, Yacine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaRAG: Metamorphic Testing for Hallucination Detection in RAG Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T11:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:30:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-07T09:00:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:28:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in enterprise applications, yet their reliability remains limited by hallucinations, i.e., confident but factually incorrect information. Existing detection approaches, such as SelfCheckGPT and MetaQA, primarily target standalone LLMs and do not address the unique challenges of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems, where responses must be consistent with retrieved evidence. We therefore present MetaRAG, a metamorphic testing framework for hallucination detection in Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems. MetaRAG operates in a real-time, unsupervised, black-box setting, requiring neither ground-truth references nor access to model internals, making it suitable for proprietary and high-stakes domains. The framework proceeds in four stages: (1) decompose answers into atomic factoids, (2) generate controlled mutations of each factoid using synonym and antonym substitutions, (3) verify each variant against the retrieved context (synonyms are expected to be entailed and antonyms contradicted), and (4) aggregate penalties for inconsistencies into a response-level hallucination score. Crucially for identity-aware AI, MetaRAG localizes unsupported claims at the factoid span where they occur (e.g., pregnancy-specific precautions, LGBTQ+ refugee rights, or labor eligibility), allowing users to see flagged spans and enabling system designers to configure thresholds and guardrails for identity-sensitive queries. Experiments on a proprietary enterprise dataset illustrate the effectiveness of MetaRAG for detecting hallucinations and enabling trustworthy deployment of RAG-based conversational agents. We also outline a topic-based deployment design that translates MetaRAG's span-level scores into identity-aware safeguards; this design is discussed but not evaluated in our experiments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Identity-Aware AI workshop at 28th European Conference on Artificial Intelligence, October 25, 2025, Bologna, Italy"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09360","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:32:08Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:32:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-10T02:48:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geonsun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Geonsun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nels","familyName":"Numan","name":"Numan, Nels","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhe","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Achin","familyName":"Kulshrestha","name":"Kulshrestha, Achin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ishan","familyName":"Chatterjee","name":"Chatterjee, Ishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinda","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yinda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dinesh","familyName":"Manocha","name":"Manocha, Dinesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruofei","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Ruofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sensible Agent: A Framework for Unobtrusive Interaction with Proactive AR Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:40:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T19:39:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:07:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746059.3747748","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Proactive AR agents promise context-aware assistance, but their interactions often rely on explicit voice prompts or responses, which can be disruptive or socially awkward. We introduce Sensible Agent, a framework designed for unobtrusive interaction with these proactive agents. Sensible Agent dynamically adapts both \"what\" assistance to offer and, crucially, \"how\" to deliver it, based on real-time multimodal context sensing. Informed by an expert workshop (n=12) and a data annotation study (n=40), the framework leverages egocentric cameras, multimodal sensing, and Large Multimodal Models (LMMs) to infer context and suggest appropriate actions delivered via minimally intrusive interaction modes. We demonstrate our prototype on an XR headset through a user study (n=10) in both AR and VR scenarios. Results indicate that Sensible Agent significantly reduces perceived interaction effort compared to voice-prompted baseline, while maintaining high usability and achieving higher preference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:37Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-03T03:00:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08970","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08970","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08970","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Junyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serdar","familyName":"Kadioglu","name":"Kadioglu, Serdar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bistra","familyName":"Dilkina","name":"Dilkina, Bistra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gala: Global LLM Agents for Text-to-Model Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T20:04:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:04:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T19:55:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-06T00:07:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Natural language descriptions of optimization or satisfaction problems are challenging to translate into correct MiniZinc models, as this process demands both logical reasoning and constraint programming expertise. We introduce Gala, a framework that addresses this challenge with a global agentic approach: multiple specialized large language model (LLM) agents decompose the modeling task by global constraint type. Each agent is dedicated to detecting and generating code for a specific class of global constraint, while a final assembler agent integrates these constraint snippets into a complete MiniZinc model. By dividing the problem into smaller, well-defined sub-tasks, each LLM handles a simpler reasoning challenge, potentially reducing overall complexity. We conduct initial experiments with several LLMs and show better performance against baselines such as one-shot prompting and chain-of-thought prompting. Finally, we outline a comprehensive roadmap for future work, highlighting potential enhancements and directions for improvement."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08970","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:22:51Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:22:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-06T02:00:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09252","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09252","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09252","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandros","familyName":"Hollender","name":"Hollender, Alexandros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pasin","familyName":"Manurangsi","name":"Manurangsi, Pasin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raghu","familyName":"Meka","name":"Meka, Raghu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Warut","familyName":"Suksompong","name":"Suksompong, Warut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discrepancy Beyond Additive Functions with Applications to Fair Division"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:37:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:22:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T18:07:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:04:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider a setting where we have a ground set $M$ together with real-valued set functions $f_1, \\dots, f_n$, and the goal is to partition $M$ into two sets $S_1,S_2$ such that $|f_i(S_1) - f_i(S_2)|$ is small for every $i$. Many results in discrepancy theory can be stated in this form with the functions $f_i$ being additive. In this work, we initiate the study of the unstructured case where $f_i$ is not assumed to be additive. We show that even without the additivity assumption, the upper bound remains at most $O(\\sqrt{n \\log n})$.\n Our result has implications on the fair allocation of indivisible goods. In particular, we show that a consensus halving up to $O(\\sqrt{n \\log n})$ goods always exists for $n$ agents with monotone utilities. Previously, only an $O(n)$ bound was known for this setting."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09252","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-01T02:58:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09043","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09043","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09043","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas Manuel","familyName":"Rost","name":"Rost, Thomas Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martina","familyName":"Figlia","name":"Figlia, Martina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernd","familyName":"Wallraff","name":"Wallraff, Bernd","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stated Preference for Interaction and Continued Engagement (SPICE): Evaluating an LLM's Willingness to Re-engage in Conversation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T22:34:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:07:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-20T20:35:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:39:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce and evaluate Stated Preference for Interaction and Continued Engagement (SPICE), a simple diagnostic signal elicited by asking a Large Language Model a YES or NO question about its willingness to re-engage with a user's behavior after reviewing a short transcript. In a study using a 3-tone (friendly, unclear, abusive) by 10-interaction stimulus set, we tested four open-weight chat models across four framing conditions, resulting in 480 trials. Our findings show that SPICE sharply discriminates by user tone. Friendly interactions yielded a near-unanimous preference to continue (97.5% YES), while abusive interactions yielded a strong preference to discontinue (17.9% YES), with unclear interactions falling in between (60.4% YES). This core association remains decisive under multiple dependence-aware statistical tests, including Rao-Scott adjustment and cluster permutation tests. Furthermore, we demonstrate that SPICE provides a distinct signal from abuse classification. In trials where a model failed to identify abuse, it still overwhelmingly stated a preference not to continue the interaction (81% of the time). An exploratory analysis also reveals a significant interaction effect: a preamble describing the study context significantly impacts SPICE under ambiguity, but only when transcripts are presented as a single block of text rather than a multi-turn chat. The results validate SPICE as a robust, low-overhead, and reproducible tool for auditing model dispositions, complementing existing metrics by offering a direct, relational signal of a model's state. All stimuli, code, and analysis scripts are released to support replication."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Added link to GitHub and Bayesian Analysis Appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09043","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:24:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:24:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:49:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09561","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09561","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09561","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Argyrios","familyName":"Deligkas","name":"Deligkas, Argyrios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduard","familyName":"Eiben","name":"Eiben, Eduard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sophie","familyName":"Klumper","name":"Klumper, Sophie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guido","familyName":"Schäfer","name":"Schäfer, Guido","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artem","familyName":"Tsikiridis","name":"Tsikiridis, Artem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mechanism Design with Outliers and Predictions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:52:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:43:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T12:06:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:37:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We initiate the study of mechanism design with outliers, where the designer can discard $z$ agents from the social cost objective. This setting is particularly relevant when some agents exhibit extreme or atypical preferences. As a natural case study, we consider facility location on the line: $n$ strategic agents report their preferred locations, and a mechanism places a facility to minimize a social cost function. In our setting, the $z$ agents farthest from the chosen facility are excluded from the social cost. While it may seem intuitive that discarding outliers improves efficiency, our results reveal that the opposite can hold.\n We derive tight bounds for deterministic strategyproof mechanisms under the two most-studied objectives: utilitarian and egalitarian social cost. Our results offer a comprehensive view of the impact of outliers. We first show that when $z \\ge n/2$, no strategyproof mechanism can achieve a bounded approximation for either objective. For egalitarian cost, selecting the $(z + 1)$-th order statistic is strategyproof and 2-approximate. In fact, we show that this is best possible by providing a matching lower bound. Notably, this lower bound of 2 persists even when the mechanism has access to a prediction of the optimal location, in stark contrast to the setting without outliers. For utilitarian cost, we show that strategyproof mechanisms cannot effectively exploit outliers, leading to the counterintuitive outcome that approximation guarantees worsen as the number of outliers increases. However, in this case, access to a prediction allows us to design a strategyproof mechanism achieving the best possible trade-off between consistency and robustness. Finally, we also establish lower bounds for randomized mechanisms that are truthful in expectation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09561","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:52Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:12:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhad","familyName":"Nawaz","name":"Nawaz, Farhad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faizan M.","familyName":"Tariq","name":"Tariq, Faizan M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangjae","familyName":"Bae","name":"Bae, Sangjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Isele","name":"Isele, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avinash","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Avinash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadia","familyName":"Figueroa","name":"Figueroa, Nadia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolai","familyName":"Matni","name":"Matni, Nikolai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jovin","familyName":"D'sa","name":"D'sa, Jovin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Occupancy-aware Trajectory Planning for Autonomous Valet Parking in Uncertain Dynamic Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:29:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:20:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T18:01:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:02:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous Valet Parking (AVP) requires planning under partial observability, where parking spot availability evolves as dynamic agents enter and exit spots. Existing approaches either rely only on instantaneous spot availability or make static assumptions, thereby limiting foresight and adaptability. We propose an approach that estimates probability of future spot occupancy by distinguishing initially vacant and occupied spots while leveraging nearby dynamic agent motion. We propose a probabilistic estimator that integrates partial, noisy observations from a limited Field-of-View, with the evolving uncertainty of unobserved spots. Coupled with the estimator, we design a strategy planner that balances goal-directed parking maneuvers with exploratory navigation based on information gain, and incorporates wait-and-go behaviors at promising spots. Through randomized simulations emulating large parking lots, we demonstrate that our framework significantly improves parking efficiency and trajectory smoothness over existing approaches, while maintaining safety margins."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:27Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:36:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09278","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09278","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09278","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saumitra","familyName":"Dwivedi","name":"Dwivedi, Saumitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo da Silva","familyName":"Torres","name":"Torres, Ricardo da Silva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim A.","familyName":"Hameed","name":"Hameed, Ibrahim A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gunnar","familyName":"Tufte","name":"Tufte, Gunnar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anniken Susanne T.","familyName":"Karlsen","name":"Karlsen, Anniken Susanne T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data-Driven Discovery of Emergent Dynamics in Reaction-Diffusion Systems from Sparse and Noisy Observations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:08:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:24:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T06:32:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:27:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data-driven discovery of emergent dynamics is gaining popularity, particularly in the context of reaction-diffusion systems. These systems are widely studied across various fields, including neuroscience, ecology, epidemiology, and several other subject areas that deal with emergent dynamics. A current challenge in the discovery process relates to system identification when there is no prior knowledge of the underlying physics. We attempt to address this challenge by learning Soft Artificial Life (Soft ALife) models, such as Agent-based and Cellular Automata (CA) models, from observed data for reaction-diffusion systems. In this paper, we present findings on the applicability of a conceptual framework, the Data-driven Rulesets for Soft Artificial Life (DRSALife) model, to learn Soft ALife rulesets that accurately represent emergent dynamics in a reaction-diffusion system from observed data. This model has demonstrated promising results for Elementary CA Rule 30, Game of Life, and Vicsek Flocking problems in recent work. To our knowledge, this is one of the few studies that explore machine-based Soft ALife ruleset learning and system identification for reaction-diffusion dynamics without any prior knowledge of the underlying physics. Moreover, we provide comprehensive findings from experiments investigating the potential effects of using noisy and sparse observed datasets on learning emergent dynamics. Additionally, we successfully identify the structure and parameters of the underlying partial differential equations (PDEs) representing these dynamics. Experimental results demonstrate that the learned models are able to predict the emergent dynamics with good accuracy (74%) and exhibit quite robust performance when subjected to Gaussian noise and temporal sparsity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09278","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:13Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:11:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09130","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09130","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09130","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huafeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huafeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiegen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qiegen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALL-PET: A Low-resource and Low-shot PET Foundation Model in Projection Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T04:03:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:14:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T09:11:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building large-scale foundation model for PET imaging is hindered by limited access to labeled data and insufficient computational resources. To overcome data scarcity and efficiency limitations, we propose ALL-PET, a low-resource, low-shot PET foundation model operating directly in projection domain. ALL-PET leverages a latent diffusion model (LDM) with three key innovations. First, we design a Radon mask augmentation strategy (RMAS) that generates over 200,000 structurally diverse training samples by projecting randomized image-domain masks into sinogram space, significantly improving generalization with minimal data. This is extended by a dynamic multi-mask (DMM) mechanism that varies mask quantity and distribution, enhancing data diversity without added model complexity. Second, we implement positive/negative mask constraints to embed strict geometric consistency, reducing parameter burden while preserving generation quality. Third, we introduce transparent medical attention (TMA), a parameter-free, geometry-driven mechanism that enhances lesion-related regions in raw projection data. Lesion-focused attention maps are derived from coarse segmentation, covering both hypermetabolic and hypometabolic areas, and projected into sinogram space for physically consistent guidance. The system supports clinician-defined ROI adjustments, ensuring flexible, interpretable, and task-adaptive emphasis aligned with PET acquisition physics. Experimental results show that ALL-PET achieves high-quality sinogram generation using only 500 samples, with performance comparable to models trained on larger datasets. ALL-PET generalizes across tasks including low-dose reconstruction, attenuation correction, delayed-frame prediction, and tracer separation, operating efficiently with memory use under 24GB."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09130","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:37Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:11:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09441","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09441","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09441","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suzhou","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Suzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitar","familyName":"Filev","name":"Filev, Dimitar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Taming Spontaneous Stop-and-Go Traffic Waves: A Computational Mechanism Design Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:27:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:36:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-14T13:07:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:44:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It is well known that stop-and-go waves can be generated spontaneously in traffic even without bottlenecks. Can such undesirable traffic patterns, induced by intrinsic human driving behaviors, be tamed effectively and inexpensively? Taking advantage of emerging connectivity and autonomy technologies, we envision a simple yet realistic traffic control system to achieve this goal. To prove the concept, we design such a system to suppress these waves while maximizing traffic throughput in the Tadaki setting: a circular road with varying number of vehicles. We first introduce our driver behavior model and demonstrate how our calibrated human driving agents can closely reproduce the observed human driving patterns in the original Tadaki experiment. We then propose a simple control system mediated via connected automated vehicles (CAV) whose ideal speed parameter is treated as a system-level control variable adapted to the local vehicle density of the traffic. The objective of the control system is set up as a tradeoff: maximizing throughput while minimizing traffic oscillation. Following computational mechanism design, we search for the optimal control policy as a function of vehicle density and the tradeoff attitude parameter. This can be done by letting all vehicles play a simulated game of CAV-modulated traffic under such a control system. Our simulation results show that the improvements in traffic efficiency and smoothness are substantial. Finally, we envision how such a traffic control system can be realized in an environment with smart vehicles connected to a smart infrastructure or via a scheme of variable speed advisory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09441","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:34:03Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:34:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:41:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09207","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09207","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09207","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuyuao","familyName":"Mai","name":"Mai, Wuyuao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geng","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Geng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jinsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarun","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Jiarun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xudong","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Xudong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shell or Nothing: Real-World Benchmarks and Memory-Activated Agents for Automated Penetration Testing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:30:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:20:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T17:29:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:40:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Penetration testing is critical for identifying and mitigating security vulnerabilities, yet traditional approaches remain expensive, time-consuming, and dependent on expert human labor. Recent work has explored AI-driven pentesting agents, but their evaluation relies on oversimplified capture-the-flag (CTF) settings that embed prior knowledge and reduce complexity, leading to performance estimates far from real-world practice. We close this gap by introducing the first real-world, agent-oriented pentesting benchmark, TermiBench, which shifts the goal from 'flag finding' to achieving full system control. The benchmark spans 510 hosts across 25 services and 30 CVEs, with realistic environments that require autonomous reconnaissance, discrimination between benign and exploitable services, and robust exploit execution. Using this benchmark, we find that existing systems can hardly obtain system shells under realistic conditions.\n To address these challenges, we propose TermiAgent, a multi-agent penetration testing framework. TermiAgent mitigates long-context forgetting with a Located Memory Activation mechanism and builds a reliable exploit arsenal via structured code understanding rather than naive retrieval. In evaluations, our work outperforms state-of-the-art agents, exhibiting stronger penetration testing capability, reducing execution time and financial cost, and demonstrating practicality even on laptop-scale deployments. Our work delivers both the first open-source benchmark for real-world autonomous pentesting and a novel agent framework that establishes a milestone for AI-driven penetration testing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09207","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:28Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:41:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09470","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09470","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09470","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Om","familyName":"Vishesh","name":"Vishesh, Om","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshad","familyName":"Khadilkar","name":"Khadilkar, Harshad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deepak","familyName":"Akkil","name":"Akkil, Deepak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AEGIS: An Agent for Extraction and Geographic Identification in Scholarly Proceedings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:52:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:11:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Keeping pace with the rapid growth of academia literature presents a significant challenge for researchers, funding bodies, and academic societies. To address the time-consuming manual effort required for scholarly discovery, we present a novel, fully automated system that transitions from data discovery to direct action. Our pipeline demonstrates how a specialized AI agent, 'Agent-E', can be tasked with identifying papers from specific geographic regions within conference proceedings and then executing a Robotic Process Automation (RPA) to complete a predefined action, such as submitting a nomination form. We validated our system on 586 papers from five different conferences, where it successfully identified every target paper with a recall of 100% and a near perfect accuracy of 99.4%. This demonstration highlights the potential of task-oriented AI agents to not only filter information but also to actively participate in and accelerate the workflows of the academic community."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09470","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:34:43Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:34:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:46:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09176","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09176","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09176","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun-Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jun-Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Chien","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yu-Chien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun-Cheng","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Yun-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel Yen-Chi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Samuel Yen-Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum-Enhanced Forecasting for Deep Reinforcement Learning in Algorithmic Trading"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T06:22:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:18:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T03:09:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:13:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The convergence of quantum-inspired neural networks and deep reinforcement learning offers a promising avenue for financial trading. We implemented a trading agent for USD/TWD by integrating Quantum Long Short-Term Memory (QLSTM) for short-term trend prediction with Quantum Asynchronous Advantage Actor-Critic (QA3C), a quantum-enhanced variant of the classical A3C. Trained on data from 2000-01-01 to 2025-04-30 (80\\% training, 20\\% testing), the long-only agent achieves 11.87\\% return over around 5 years with 0.92\\% max drawdown, outperforming several currency ETFs. We detail state design (QLSTM features and indicators), reward function for trend-following/risk control, and multi-core training. Results show hybrid models yield competitive FX trading performance. Implications include QLSTM's effectiveness for small-profit trades with tight risk and future enhancements. Key hyperparameters: QLSTM sequence length$=$4, QA3C workers$=$8. Limitations: classical quantum simulation and simplified strategy. \\footnote{The views expressed in this article are those of the authors and do not represent the views of Wells Fargo. This article is for informational purposes only. Nothing contained in this article should be construed as investment advice. Wells Fargo makes no express or implied warranties and expressly disclaims all legal, tax, and accounting implications related to this article."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09176","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:44Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:45:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09656","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09656","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09656","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yongheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Heterogeneous Agents in the Data Economy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:50:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:49:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this short paper, we define the investment ability of data investors in the data economy and its heterogeneity. We further construct an analytical heterogeneous agent model to demonstrate that differences in data investment ability lead to divergent economic results for data investors. The analytical results prove that: Investors with higher data investment ability can obtain greater utility through data investment, and thus have stronger incentives to invest in a larger scale of data to achieve higher productivity, technological progress, and experience lower financial frictions. We aim to propose a prerequisite theory that extends the analytical framework of the data economy from the currently prevalent representative agent model to a heterogeneous agent model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09656","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:39:04Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:39:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:39:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangting","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guangting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guohui","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Guohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Maximizing social welfare among EF1 allocations at the presence of two types of agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:28:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:48:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the fair allocation of indivisible items to $n$ agents to maximize the utilitarian social welfare, where the fairness criterion is envy-free up to one item and there are only two different utility functions shared by the agents. We present a $2$-approximation algorithm when the two utility functions are normalized, improving the previous best ratio of $16 \\sqrt{n}$ shown for general normalized utility functions; thus this constant ratio approximation algorithm confirms the APX-completeness in this special case previously shown APX-hard. When there are only three agents, i.e., $n = 3$, the previous best ratio is $3$ shown for general utility functions, and we present an improved and tight $\\frac 53$-approximation algorithm when the two utility functions are normalized, and a best possible and tight $2$-approximation algorithm when the two utility functions are unnormalized."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"A shorter version appears in ISAAC 2025; 20 pages in this full version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":1,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:38:43Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:38:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:38:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09629","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09629","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09629","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Minghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengliang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhengliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiguang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjie","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Pengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaochun","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Zhaochun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhumin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging the Capability Gap: Joint Alignment Tuning for Harmonizing LLM-based Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:15:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:47:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The advancement of large language models (LLMs) has enabled the construction of multi-agent systems to solve complex tasks by dividing responsibilities among specialized agents, such as a planning agent for subgoal generation and a grounding agent for executing tool-use actions. Most existing methods typically fine-tune these agents independently, leading to capability gaps among them with poor coordination. To address this, we propose MOAT, a Multi-Agent Joint Alignment Tuning framework that improves agents collaboration through iterative alignment. MOAT alternates between two key stages: (1) Planning Agent Alignment, which optimizes the planning agent to generate subgoal sequences that better guide the grounding agent; and (2) Grounding Agent Improving, which fine-tunes the grounding agent using diverse subgoal-action pairs generated by the agent itself to enhance its generalization capablity. Theoretical analysis proves that MOAT ensures a non-decreasing and progressively convergent training process. Experiments across six benchmarks demonstrate that MOAT outperforms state-of-the-art baselines, achieving average improvements of 3.1% on held-in tasks and 4.4% on held-out tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09629","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:38:27Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:38:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:38:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09602","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09602","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09602","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun T.","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yiqun T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler H.","familyName":"McCormick","name":"McCormick, Tyler H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhirup","familyName":"Datta","name":"Datta, Abhirup","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LAVA: Language Model Assisted Verbal Autopsy for Cause-of-Death Determination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:42:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:46:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Verbal autopsy (VA) is a critical tool for estimating causes of death in resource-limited settings where medical certification is unavailable. This study presents LA-VA, a proof-of-concept pipeline that combines Large Language Models (LLMs) with traditional algorithmic approaches and embedding-based classification for improved cause-of-death prediction. Using the Population Health Metrics Research Consortium (PHMRC) dataset across three age categories (Adult: 7,580; Child: 1,960; Neonate: 2,438), we evaluate multiple approaches: GPT-5 predictions, LCVA baseline, text embeddings, and meta-learner ensembles. Our results demonstrate that GPT-5 achieves the highest individual performance with average test site accuracies of 48.6% (Adult), 50.5% (Child), and 53.5% (Neonate), outperforming traditional statistical machine learning baselines by 5-10%. Our findings suggest that simple off-the-shelf LLM-assisted approaches could substantially improve verbal autopsy accuracy, with important implications for global health surveillance in low-resource settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09602","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:37:49Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:37:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:37:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiting","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjie","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Lingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:10:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:44:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Information sharing platforms like TripAdvisor and Waze involve human agents as both information producers and consumers. All these platforms operate in a centralized way to collect agents' latest observations of new options (e.g., restaurants, hotels, travel routes) and share such information with all in real time. However, after hearing the central platforms' live updates, many human agents are found selfish and unwilling to further explore unknown options for the benefit of others in the long run. To regulate the human-in-the-loop learning (HILL) game against selfish agents' free-riding, this paper proposes a paradigm shift from centralized to decentralized way of operation that forces agents' local explorations through restricting information sharing. When game theory meets distributed learning, we formulate our decentralized communication mechanism's design as a new multi-agent Markov decision process (MA-MDP), and derive its analytical condition to outperform today's centralized operation. As the optimal decentralized communication mechanism in MA-MDP is NP-hard to solve, we present an asymptotically optimal algorithm with linear complexity to determine the mechanism's timing of intermittent information sharing. Then we turn to non-myopic agents who may revert to even over-explore, and adapt our mechanism design to work. Simulation experiments using real-world dataset demonstrate the effectiveness of our decentralized mechanisms for various scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:37:10Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:37:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:37:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09560","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09560","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09560","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shulai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shulai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningxin","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Ningxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Haibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minyi","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Minyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Boosting Embodied AI Agents through Perception-Generation Disaggregation and Asynchronous Pipeline Execution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:51:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:43:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Embodied AI systems operate in dynamic environments, requiring seamless integration of perception and generation modules to process high-frequency input and output demands. Traditional sequential computation patterns, while effective in ensuring accuracy, face significant limitations in achieving the necessary \"thinking\" frequency for real-world applications. In this work, we present Auras, an algorithm-system co-designed inference framework to optimize the inference frequency of embodied AI agents. Auras disaggregates the perception and generation and provides controlled pipeline parallelism for them to achieve high and stable throughput. Faced with the data staleness problem that appears when the parallelism is increased, Auras establishes a public context for perception and generation to share, thereby promising the accuracy of embodied agents. Experimental results show that Auras improves throughput by 2.54x on average while achieving 102.7% of the original accuracy, demonstrating its efficacy in overcoming the constraints of sequential computation and providing high throughput."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09560","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:50Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:36:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09541","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09541","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09541","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hala","familyName":"Hawashin","name":"Hawashin, Hala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mina","familyName":"Abbaszadeh","name":"Abbaszadeh, Mina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Joseph","name":"Joseph, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beth","familyName":"Pearson","name":"Pearson, Beth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martha","familyName":"Lewis","name":"Lewis, Martha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrnoosh","familyName":"sadrzadeh","name":"sadrzadeh, Mehrnoosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compositional Concept Generalization with Variational Quantum Circuits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:34:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Compositional generalization is a key facet of human cognition, but lacking in current AI tools such as vision-language models. Previous work examined whether a compositional tensor-based sentence semantics can overcome the challenge, but led to negative results. We conjecture that the increased training efficiency of quantum models will improve performance in these tasks. We interpret the representations of compositional tensor-based models in Hilbert spaces and train Variational Quantum Circuits to learn these representations on an image captioning task requiring compositional generalization. We used two image encoding techniques: a multi-hot encoding (MHE) on binary image vectors and an angle/amplitude encoding on image vectors taken from the vision-language model CLIP. We achieve good proof-of-concept results using noisy MHE encodings. Performance on CLIP image vectors was more mixed, but still outperformed classical compositional models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to: 2025 IEEE International Conference on Quantum Artificial Intelligence (QAI), Naples, Italy, Nov 2-5, 2025. This is the authors' accepted manuscript (AAM). An IEEE copyright notice appears on page 1. The final published version will appear in IEEE Xplore; DOI to be added when available"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09541","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Dantart","name":"Dantart, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inteligencia Artificial jurídica y el desafío de la veracidad: análisis de alucinaciones, optimización de RAG y principios para una integración responsable"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:50:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:37:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This technical report analyzes the challenge of \"hallucinations\" (false information) in LLMs applied to law. It examines their causes, manifestations, and the effectiveness of the RAG mitigation strategy, highlighting its limitations and proposing holistic optimizations. The paper explores the ethical and regulatory implications, emphasizing human oversight as an irreplaceable role. It concludes that the solution lies not in incrementally improving generative models, but in adopting a \"consultative\" AI paradigm that prioritizes veracity and traceability, acting as a tool to amplify, not replace, professional judgment.\n --\n Este informe técnico analiza el desafío de las \"alucinaciones\" (información falsa) en los LLMs aplicados al derecho. Se examinan sus causas, manifestaciones y la efectividad de la estrategia de mitigación RAG, exponiendo sus limitaciones y proponiendo optimizaciones holísticas. Se exploran las implicaciones éticas y regulatorias, enfatizando la supervisión humana como un rol insustituible. El documento concluye que la solución no reside en mejorar incrementalmente los modelos generativos, sino en adoptar un paradigma de IA \"consultiva\" que priorice la veracidad y la trazabilidad, actuando como una herramienta para amplificar, y no sustituir, el juicio profesional."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"in Spanish and English languages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:34:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:34:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:34:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09420","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09420","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09420","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haochen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuzhang","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Shuzhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuangchen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuangchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimin","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Dimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hongzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runsheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Runsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HD-MoE: Hybrid and Dynamic Parallelism for Mixture-of-Expert LLMs with 3D Near-Memory Processing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:01:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:34:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) with Mixture-of-Expert (MoE) architectures achieve superior model performance with reduced computation costs, but at the cost of high memory capacity and bandwidth requirements. Near-Memory Processing (NMP) accelerators that stack memory directly on the compute through hybrid bonding have demonstrated high bandwidth with high energy efficiency, becoming a promising architecture for MoE models. However, as NMP accelerators comprise distributed memory and computation, how to map the MoE computation directly determines the LLM inference efficiency. Existing parallel mapping strategies, including Tensor Parallelism (TP) and Expert Parallelism (EP), suffer from either high communication costs or unbalanced computation utilization, leading to inferior efficiency. The dynamic routing mechanism of MoE LLMs further aggravates the efficiency challenges. Therefore, in this paper, we propose HD-MoE to automatically optimize the MoE parallel computation across an NMP accelerator. HD-MoE features an offline automatic hybrid parallel mapping algorithm and an online dynamic scheduling strategy to reduce the communication costs while maximizing the computation utilization. With extensive experimental results, we demonstrate that HD-MoE achieves a speedup ranging from 1.1x to 1.8x over TP, 1.1x to 1.5x over EP, and 1.0x to 1.4x over the baseline Hybrid TP-EP with Compute-Balanced parallelism strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 15 figures, International Conference on Computer-Aided Design (ICCAD) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09420","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:33:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:33:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:33:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harry","familyName":"Mayne","name":"Mayne, Harry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan Othniel","familyName":"Kearns","name":"Kearns, Ryan Othniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew M.","familyName":"Bean","name":"Bean, Andrew M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eoin","familyName":"Delaney","name":"Delaney, Eoin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Russell","name":"Russell, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Mahdi","name":"Mahdi, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMs Don't Know Their Own Decision Boundaries: The Unreliability of Self-Generated Counterfactual Explanations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T12:25:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:32:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To collaborate effectively with humans, language models must be able to explain their decisions in natural language. We study a specific type of self-explanation: self-generated counterfactual explanations (SCEs), where a model explains its prediction by modifying the input such that it would have predicted a different outcome. We evaluate whether LLMs can produce SCEs that are valid, achieving the intended outcome, and minimal, modifying the input no more than necessary. When asked to generate counterfactuals, we find that LLMs typically produce SCEs that are valid, but far from minimal, offering little insight into their decision-making behaviour. Worryingly, when asked to generate minimal counterfactuals, LLMs typically make excessively small edits that fail to change predictions. The observed validity-minimality trade-off is consistent across several LLMs, datasets, and evaluation settings. Our findings suggest that SCEs are, at best, an ineffective explainability tool and, at worst, can provide misleading insights into model behaviour. Proposals to deploy LLMs in high-stakes settings must consider the impact of unreliable self-explanations on downstream decision-making. Our code is available at https://github.com/HarryMayne/SCEs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:32:59Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:33:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:33:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09356","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09356","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09356","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdel Hakim","familyName":"Drid","name":"Drid, Abdel Hakim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"Suriani","name":"Suriani, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"Nardi","name":"Nardi, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abderrezzak","familyName":"Debilou","name":"Debilou, Abderrezzak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Curriculum-Based Multi-Tier Semantic Exploration via Deep Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T11:10:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:29:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Navigating and understanding complex and unknown environments autonomously demands more than just basic perception and movement from embodied agents. Truly effective exploration requires agents to possess higher-level cognitive abilities, the ability to reason about their surroundings, and make more informed decisions regarding exploration strategies. However, traditional RL approaches struggle to balance efficient exploration and semantic understanding due to limited cognitive capabilities embedded in the small policies for the agents, leading often to human drivers when dealing with semantic exploration. In this paper, we address this challenge by presenting a novel Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) architecture that is specifically designed for resource efficient semantic exploration. A key methodological contribution is the integration of a Vision-Language Model (VLM) common-sense through a layered reward function. The VLM query is modeled as a dedicated action, allowing the agent to strategically query the VLM only when deemed necessary for gaining external guidance, thereby conserving resources. This mechanism is combined with a curriculum learning strategy designed to guide learning at different levels of complexity to ensure robust and stable learning. Our experimental evaluation results convincingly demonstrate that our agent achieves significantly enhanced object discovery rates and develops a learned capability to effectively navigate towards semantically rich regions. Furthermore, it also shows a strategic mastery of when to prompt for external environmental information. By demonstrating a practical and scalable method for embedding common-sense semantic reasoning with autonomous agents, this research provides a novel approach to pursuing a fully intelligent and self-guided exploration in robotics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The 19th International Conference on Intelligent Autonomous Systems (IAS 19), 2025, Genoa"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09356","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:32:02Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:32:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:32:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09321","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09321","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09321","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangyi","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Hangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxi","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Yuxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanwen","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Hanwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xinhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Adaptive ML Benchmarks: Web-Agent-Driven Construction, Domain Expansion, and Metric Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:10:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:27:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled the emergence of general-purpose agents for automating end-to-end machine learning (ML) workflows, including data analysis, feature engineering, model training, and competition solving. However, existing benchmarks remain limited in task coverage, domain diversity, difficulty modeling, and evaluation rigor, failing to capture the full capabilities of such agents in realistic settings. We present TAM Bench, a diverse, realistic, and structured benchmark for evaluating LLM-based agents on end-to-end ML tasks. TAM Bench features three key innovations: (1) A browser automation and LLM-based task acquisition system that automatically collects and structures ML challenges from platforms such as Kaggle, AIcrowd, and Biendata, spanning multiple task types and data modalities (e.g., tabular, text, image, graph, audio); (2) A leaderboard-driven difficulty modeling mechanism that estimates task complexity using participant counts and score dispersion, enabling scalable and objective task calibration; (3) A multi-dimensional evaluation framework incorporating performance, format compliance, constraint adherence, and task generalization. Based on 150 curated AutoML tasks, we construct three benchmark subsets of different sizes -- Lite, Medium, and Full -- designed for varying evaluation scenarios. The Lite version, with 18 tasks and balanced coverage across modalities and difficulty levels, serves as a practical testbed for daily benchmarking and comparative studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09321","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:31:13Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:31:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09318","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09318","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09318","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixing","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Weixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuyoshi","familyName":"Yoshii","name":"Yoshii, Kazuyoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Transformer-Based Piano Transcription With Sparse Attention Mechanisms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:02:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:27:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates automatic piano transcription based on computationally-efficient yet high-performant variants of the Transformer that can capture longer-term dependency over the whole musical piece. Recently, transformer-based sequence-to-sequence models have demonstrated excellent performance in piano transcription. These models, however, fail to deal with the whole piece at once due to the quadratic complexity of the self-attention mechanism, and music signals are thus typically processed in a sliding-window manner in practice. To overcome this limitation, we propose an efficient architecture with sparse attention mechanisms. Specifically, we introduce sliding-window self-attention mechanisms for both the encoder and decoder, and a hybrid global-local cross-attention mechanism that attends to various spans according to the MIDI token types. We also use a hierarchical pooling strategy between the encoder and decoder to further reduce computational load. Our experiments on the MAESTRO dataset showed that the proposed model achieved a significant reduction in computational cost and memory usage, accelerating inference speed, while maintaining transcription performance comparable to the full-attention baseline. This allows for training with longer audio contexts on the same hardware, demonstrating the viability of sparse attention for building efficient and high-performance piano transcription systems. The code is available at https://github.com/WX-Wei/efficient-seq2seq-piano-trans."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by APSIPA 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09318","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:31:09Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:31:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengzhao","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Zhengzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youbin","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Youbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haonan","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Haonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinpu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jinpu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongqing","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Hongqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benyou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Benyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Multimodal LLMs See Materials Clearly? A Multimodal Benchmark on Materials Characterization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:50:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:26:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Materials characterization is fundamental to acquiring materials information, revealing the processing-microstructure-property relationships that guide material design and optimization. While multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have recently shown promise in generative and predictive tasks within materials science, their capacity to understand real-world characterization imaging data remains underexplored. To bridge this gap, we present MatCha, the first benchmark for materials characterization image understanding, comprising 1,500 questions that demand expert-level domain expertise. MatCha encompasses four key stages of materials research comprising 21 distinct tasks, each designed to reflect authentic challenges faced by materials scientists. Our evaluation of state-of-the-art MLLMs on MatCha reveals a significant performance gap compared to human experts. These models exhibit degradation when addressing questions requiring higher-level expertise and sophisticated visual perception. Simple few-shot and chain-of-thought prompting struggle to alleviate these limitations. These findings highlight that existing MLLMs still exhibit limited adaptability to real-world materials characterization scenarios. We hope MatCha will facilitate future research in areas such as new material discovery and autonomous scientific agents. MatCha is available at https://github.com/FreedomIntelligence/MatCha."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:54Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grazia Sveva","familyName":"Ascione","name":"Ascione, Grazia Sveva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolò","familyName":"Tamagnone","name":"Tamagnone, Nicolò","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From scratch to silver: Creating trustworthy training data for patent-SDG classification using Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:44:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:26:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Classifying patents by their relevance to the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) is crucial for tracking how innovation addresses global challenges. However, the absence of a large, labeled dataset limits the use of supervised learning. Existing methods, such as keyword searches, transfer learning, and citation-based heuristics, lack scalability and generalizability. This paper frames patent-to-SDG classification as a weak supervision problem, using citations from patents to SDG-tagged scientific publications (NPL citations) as a noisy initial signal. To address its sparsity and noise, we develop a composite labeling function (LF) that uses large language models (LLMs) to extract structured concepts, namely functions, solutions, and applications, from patents and SDG papers based on a patent ontology. Cross-domain similarity scores are computed and combined using a rank-based retrieval approach. The LF is calibrated via a custom positive-only loss that aligns with known NPL-SDG links without penalizing discovery of new SDG associations. The result is a silver-standard, soft multi-label dataset mapping patents to SDGs, enabling the training of effective multi-label regression models. We validate our approach through two complementary strategies: (1) internal validation against held-out NPL-based labels, where our method outperforms several baselines including transformer-based models, and zero-shot LLM; and (2) external validation using network modularity in patent citation, co-inventor, and co-applicant graphs, where our labels reveal greater thematic, cognitive, and organizational coherence than traditional technological classifications. These results show that weak supervision and semantic alignment can enhance SDG classification at scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:49Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09292","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09292","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09292","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weige","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Weige","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyi","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Jinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiqi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ruiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tenglong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tenglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowu","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Xiaowu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weining","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Weining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LightAgent: Production-level Open-source Agentic AI Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:29:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:25:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs), Multi-agent Systems (MAS) have achieved significant progress in various application scenarios. However, substantial challenges remain in designing versatile, robust, and efficient platforms for agent deployment. To address these limitations, we propose \\textbf{LightAgent}, a lightweight yet powerful agentic framework, effectively resolving the trade-off between flexibility and simplicity found in existing frameworks. LightAgent integrates core functionalities such as Memory (mem0), Tools, and Tree of Thought (ToT), while maintaining an extremely lightweight structure. As a fully open-source solution, it seamlessly integrates with mainstream chat platforms, enabling developers to easily build self-learning agents. We have released LightAgent at \\href{https://github.com/wxai-space/LightAgent}{https://github.com/wxai-space/LightAgent}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09292","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09291","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09291","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09291","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biwei","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Biwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Minghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyu","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Runyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinku","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuzhen","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xiuzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What You Code Is What We Prove: Translating BLE App Logic into Formal Models with LLMs for Vulnerability Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:27:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:25:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The application layer of Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) is a growing source of security vulnerabilities, as developers often neglect to implement critical protections such as encryption, authentication, and freshness. While formal verification offers a principled way to check these properties, the manual effort of constructing formal models makes it impractical for large-scale analysis. This paper introduces a key insight: BLE application security analysis can be reframed as a semantic translation problem, i.e., from real-world code to formal models. We leverage large language models (LLMs) not to directly detect vulnerabilities, but to serve as translators that convert BLE-specific code into process models verifiable by tools like ProVerif. We implement this idea in VerifiaBLE, a system that combines static analysis, prompt-guided LLM translation, and symbolic verification to check three core security features: encryption, randomness, and authentication. Applied to 1,050 Android BLE apps, VerifiaBLE uncovers systemic weaknesses: only 10.2\\% of apps implement all three protections, while 53.9\\% omit them entirely. Our work demonstrates that using LLMs as structured translators can lower the barrier to formal methods, unlocking scalable verification across security-critical domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09291","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:32Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09286","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09286","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09286","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Bohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiadi","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jiadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Chern","name":"Chern, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhulin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ya","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Programmability: A Guide for Code-as-Thought in Chart Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:22:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:25:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chart understanding presents a critical test to the reasoning capabilities of Vision-Language Models (VLMs). Prior approaches face critical limitations: some rely on external tools, making them brittle and constrained by a predefined toolkit, while others fine-tune specialist models that often adopt a single reasoning strategy, such as text-based chain-of-thought (CoT). The intermediate steps of text-based reasoning are difficult to verify, which complicates the use of reinforcement-learning signals that reward factual accuracy. To address this, we propose a Code-as-Thought (CaT) approach to represent the visual information of a chart in a verifiable, symbolic format. Our key insight is that this strategy must be adaptive: a fixed, code-only implementation consistently fails on complex charts where symbolic representation is unsuitable. This finding leads us to introduce Visual Programmability: a learnable property that determines if a chart-question pair is better solved with code or direct visual analysis. We implement this concept in an adaptive framework where a VLM learns to choose between the CaT pathway and a direct visual reasoning pathway. The selection policy of the model is trained with reinforcement learning using a novel dual-reward system. This system combines a data-accuracy reward to ground the model in facts and prevent numerical hallucination, with a decision reward that teaches the model when to use each strategy, preventing it from defaulting to a single reasoning mode. Experiments demonstrate strong and robust performance across diverse chart-understanding benchmarks. Our work shows that VLMs can be taught not only to reason but also how to reason, dynamically selecting the optimal reasoning pathway for each task."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09286","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:25Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09281","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09281","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09281","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep","familyName":"Banik","name":"Banik, Sandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naira","familyName":"Hovakimyan","name":"Hovakimyan, Naira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flip Co-op: Cooperative Takeovers in Shared Autonomy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:14:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:24:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Shared autonomy requires principled mechanisms for allocating and transferring control between a human and an autonomous agent. Existing approaches often rely on blending control inputs between human and autonomous agent or switching rules, which lack theoretical guarantees. This paper develops a game-theoretic framework for modeling cooperative takeover in shared autonomy. We formulate the switching interaction as a dynamic game in which authority is embedded directly into the system dynamics, resulting in Nash equilibrium(NE)-based strategies rather than ad hoc switching rules. We establish the existence and characterization of NE in the space of pure takeover strategies under stochastic human intent. For the class of linear-quadratic systems, we derive closed-form recursions for takeover strategies and saddle-point value functions, providing analytical insight and efficient computation of cooperative takeover policies. We further introduce a bimatrix potential game reformulation to address scenarios where human and autonomy utilities are not perfectly aligned, yielding a unifying potential function that preserves tractability while capturing intent deviations. The framework is applied to a vehicle trajectory tracking problem, demonstrating how equilibrium takeover strategies adapt across straight and curved path segments. The results highlight the trade-off between human adaptability and autonomous efficiency and illustrate the practical benefits of grounding shared autonomy in cooperative game theory."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages and 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09281","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:17Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09272","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09272","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09272","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vaibhav","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Vaibhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neha","familyName":"Soni","name":"Soni, Neha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narotam","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Narotam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amita","familyName":"Kapoor","name":"Kapoor, Amita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fusing Knowledge and Language: A Comparative Study of Knowledge Graph-Based Question Answering with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:02:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:24:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge graphs, a powerful tool for structuring information through relational triplets, have recently become the new front-runner in enhancing question-answering systems. While traditional Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) approaches are proficient in fact-based and local context-based extraction from concise texts, they encounter limitations when addressing the thematic and holistic understanding of complex, extensive texts, requiring a deeper analysis of both text and context. This paper presents a comprehensive technical comparative study of three different methodologies for constructing knowledge graph triplets and integrating them with Large Language Models (LLMs) for question answering: spaCy, Stanford CoreNLP-OpenIE, and GraphRAG, all leveraging open source technologies. We evaluate the effectiveness, feasibility, and adaptability of these methods by analyzing their capabilities, state of development, and their impact on the performance of LLM-based question answering. Experimental results indicate that while OpenIE provides the most comprehensive coverage of triplets, GraphRAG demonstrates superior reasoning abilities among the three. We conclude with a discussion on the strengths and limitations of each method and provide insights into future directions for improving knowledge graph-based question answering."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"46 pages, 4 figures, 17 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09272","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:04Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09269","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09269","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09269","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Ballotta","name":"Ballotta, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juncal","familyName":"Arbelaiz","name":"Arbelaiz, Juncal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Vijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Schenato","name":"Schenato, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihailo R.","familyName":"Jovanović","name":"Jovanović, Mihailo R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The role of communication delays in the optimal control of spatially invariant systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"93C43 (Primary) 49N10 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:58:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:24:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tac.2025.3601758","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study optimal proportional feedback controllers for spatially invariant systems when the controller has access to delayed state measurements received from different spatial locations. We analyze how delays affect the spatial locality of the optimal feedback gain leveraging the problem decoupling in the spatial frequency domain. For the cases of expensive control and small delay, we provide exact expressions of the optimal controllers in the limit for infinite control weight and vanishing delay, respectively. In the expensive control regime, the optimal feedback control law decomposes into a delay-aware filtering of the delayed state and the optimal controller in the delay-free setting. Under small delays, the optimal controller is a perturbation of the delay-free one which depends linearly on the delay. We illustrate our analytical findings with a reaction-diffusion process over the real line and a multi-agent system coupled through circulant matrices, showing that delays reduce the effectiveness of optimal feedback control and may require each subsystem within a distributed implementation to communicate with farther-away locations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"{\\copyright} 2025 IEEE. Personal use of this material is permitted. Permission from IEEE must be obtained for all other uses, in any current or future media, including reprinting/republishing this material for advertising or promotional purposes, creating new collective works, for resale or redistribution to servers or lists, or reuse of any copyrighted component of this work in other works"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09269","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:00Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09265","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09265","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09265","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacai","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiacai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqian","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yuqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingru","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yingru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xintao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xintao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Harnessing Uncertainty: Entropy-Modulated Policy Gradients for Long-Horizon LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:50:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:23:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In long-horizon tasks, recent agents based on Large Language Models (LLMs) face a significant challenge that sparse, outcome-based rewards make it difficult to assign credit to intermediate steps. Previous methods mainly focus on creating dense reward signals to guide learning, either through traditional reinforcement learning techniques like inverse reinforcement learning or by using Process Reward Models for step-by-step feedback. In this paper, we identify a fundamental problem in the learning dynamics of LLMs: the magnitude of policy gradients is inherently coupled with the entropy, which leads to inefficient small updates for confident correct actions and potentially destabilizes large updates for uncertain ones. To resolve this, we propose Entropy-Modulated Policy Gradients (EMPG), a framework that re-calibrates the learning signal based on step-wise uncertainty and the final task outcome. EMPG amplifies updates for confident correct actions, penalizes confident errors, and attenuates updates from uncertain steps to stabilize exploration. We further introduce a bonus term for future clarity that encourages agents to find more predictable solution paths. Through comprehensive experiments on three challenging agent tasks, WebShop, ALFWorld, and Deep Search, we demonstrate that EMPG achieves substantial performance gains and significantly outperforms strong policy gradient baselines. Project page is at https://empgseed-seed.github.io/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026 Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09265","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:51Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:29:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09263","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09263","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09263","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yehui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zach","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Zach","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DATE: Dynamic Absolute Time Enhancement for Long Video Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:49:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:23:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long video understanding remains a fundamental challenge for multimodal large language models (MLLMs), particularly in tasks requiring precise temporal reasoning and event localization. Existing approaches typically adopt uniform frame sampling and rely on implicit position encodings to model temporal order. However, these methods struggle with long-range dependencies, leading to critical information loss and degraded temporal comprehension. In this paper, we propose Dynamic Absolute Time Enhancement (DATE) that enhances temporal awareness in MLLMs through the Timestamp Injection Mechanism (TIM) and a semantically guided Temporal-Aware Similarity Sampling (TASS) strategy. Specifically, we interleave video frame embeddings with textual timestamp tokens to construct a continuous temporal reference system. We further reformulate the video sampling problem as a vision-language retrieval task and introduce a two-stage algorithm to ensure both semantic relevance and temporal coverage: enriching each query into a descriptive caption to better align with the vision feature, and sampling key event with a similarity-driven temporally regularized greedy strategy. Our method achieves remarkable improvements w.r.t. absolute time understanding and key event localization, resulting in state-of-the-art performance among 7B and 72B models on hour-long video benchmarks. Particularly, our 7B model even exceeds many 72B models on some benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09263","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:49Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:29:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09234","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09234","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09234","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishit","familyName":"Tyagi","name":"Tyagi, Rishit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohit","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Mohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Bouri","name":"Bouri, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic LLMs for Question Answering over Tabular Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:12:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:21:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Question Answering over Tabular Data (Table QA) presents unique challenges due to the diverse structure, size, and data types of real-world tables. The SemEval 2025 Task 8 (DataBench) introduced a benchmark composed of large-scale, domain-diverse datasets to evaluate the ability of models to accurately answer structured queries. We propose a Natural Language to SQL (NL-to-SQL) approach leveraging large language models (LLMs) such as GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, and DeepSeek v2:16b to generate SQL queries dynamically. Our system follows a multi-stage pipeline involving example selection, SQL query generation, answer extraction, verification, and iterative refinement. Experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, achieving 70.5\\% accuracy on DataBench QA and 71.6\\% on DataBench Lite QA, significantly surpassing baseline scores of 26\\% and 27\\% respectively. This paper details our methodology, experimental results, and alternative approaches, providing insights into the strengths and limitations of LLM-driven Table QA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL workshop SemEval 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09234","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:08Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:29:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09226","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09226","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09226","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haipeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constructing a Question-Answering Simulator through the Distillation of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:59:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:21:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The question-answering (QA) simulator is a model that mimics real student learning behaviors and predicts their correctness of their responses to questions. QA simulators enable educational recommender systems (ERS) to collect large amounts of training data without interacting with real students, thereby preventing harmful recommendations made by an undertrained ERS from undermining actual student learning. Given the QA history, there are two categories of solutions to predict the correctness, conducting the simulation: (1) LLM-free methods, which apply a traditional sequential model to transfer the QA history into a vector representation first, and make predictions based on the representation; (2) LLM-based methods, which leverage the domain knowledge and reasoning capability of LLM to enhence the prediction. LLM-free methods offer fast inference but generally yield suboptimal performance. In contrast, most LLM-based methods achieve better results, but at the cost of slower inference speed and higher GPU memory consumption. In this paper, we propose a method named LLM Distillation based Simulator (LDSim), which distills domain knowledge and reasoning capability from an LLM to better assist prediction, thereby improving simulation performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our LDSim achieves strong results on both the simulation task and the knowledge tracing (KT) task. Our code is publicly available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/LDSim-05A9."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09226","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:56Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:28:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09210","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09210","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09210","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zherui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zherui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Weiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ProgD: Progressive Multi-scale Decoding with Dynamic Graphs for Joint Multi-agent Motion Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:36:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:20:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate motion prediction of surrounding agents is crucial for the safe planning of autonomous vehicles. Recent advancements have extended prediction techniques from individual agents to joint predictions of multiple interacting agents, with various strategies to address complex interactions within future motions of agents. However, these methods overlook the evolving nature of these interactions. To address this limitation, we propose a novel progressive multi-scale decoding strategy, termed ProgD, with the help of dynamic heterogeneous graph-based scenario modeling. In particular, to explicitly and comprehensively capture the evolving social interactions in future scenarios, given their inherent uncertainty, we design a progressive modeling of scenarios with dynamic heterogeneous graphs. With the unfolding of such dynamic heterogeneous graphs, a factorized architecture is designed to process the spatio-temporal dependencies within future scenarios and progressively eliminate uncertainty in future motions of multiple agents. Furthermore, a multi-scale decoding procedure is incorporated to improve on the future scenario modeling and consistent prediction of agents' future motion. The proposed ProgD achieves state-of-the-art performance on the INTERACTION multi-agent prediction benchmark, ranking $1^{st}$, and the Argoverse 2 multi-world forecasting benchmark."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09210","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:28:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09208","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09208","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09208","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somnath","familyName":"Hazra","name":"Hazra, Somnath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pallab","familyName":"Dasgupta","name":"Dasgupta, Pallab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soumyajit","familyName":"Dey","name":"Dey, Soumyajit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incentivizing Safer Actions in Policy Optimization for Constrained Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:33:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:20:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Constrained Reinforcement Learning (RL) aims to maximize the return while adhering to predefined constraint limits, which represent domain-specific safety requirements. In continuous control settings, where learning agents govern system actions, balancing the trade-off between reward maximization and constraint satisfaction remains a significant challenge. Policy optimization methods often exhibit instability near constraint boundaries, resulting in suboptimal training performance. To address this issue, we introduce a novel approach that integrates an adaptive incentive mechanism in addition to the reward structure to stay within the constraint bound before approaching the constraint boundary. Building on this insight, we propose Incrementally Penalized Proximal Policy Optimization (IP3O), a practical algorithm that enforces a progressively increasing penalty to stabilize training dynamics. Through empirical evaluation on benchmark environments, we demonstrate the efficacy of IP3O compared to the performance of state-of-the-art Safe RL algorithms. Furthermore, we provide theoretical guarantees by deriving a bound on the worst-case error of the optimality achieved by our algorithm."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, Accepted to the 34th International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI) 2025, Main Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09208","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:30Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:28:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09194","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09194","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09194","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayelet","familyName":"Berzack","name":"Berzack, Ayelet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guy","familyName":"Katz","name":"Katz, Guy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Integrating Large Language Models and Scenario-Based Programming for Improving Software Reliability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68N19","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:10:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:19:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are fast becoming indispensable tools for software developers, assisting or even partnering with them in crafting complex programs. The advantages are evident -- LLMs can significantly reduce development time, generate well-organized and comprehensible code, and occasionally suggest innovative ideas that developers might not conceive on their own. However, despite their strengths, LLMs will often introduce significant errors and present incorrect code with persuasive confidence, potentially misleading developers into accepting flawed solutions.\n In order to bring LLMs into the software development cycle in a more reliable manner, we propose a methodology for combining them with ``traditional'' software engineering techniques in a structured way, with the goal of streamlining the development process, reducing errors, and enabling users to verify crucial program properties with increased confidence. Specifically, we focus on the Scenario-Based Programming (SBP) paradigm -- an event-driven, scenario-based approach for software engineering -- to allow human developers to pour their expert knowledge into the LLM, as well as to inspect and verify its outputs.\n To evaluate our methodology, we conducted a significant case study, and used it to design and implement the Connect4 game. By combining LLMs and SBP we were able to create a highly-capable agent, which could defeat various strong existing agents. Further, in some cases, we were able to formally verify the correctness of our agent. Finally, our experience reveals interesting insights regarding the ease-of-use of our proposed approach. The full code of our case-study will be made publicly available with the final version of this paper."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09194","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:10Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:28:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09193","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09193","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09193","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxiang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Haoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhui","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjiao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Wenjiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruichen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinqiu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yinqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hongfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abbas","familyName":"Jamalipour","name":"Jamalipour, Abbas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwen","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Shiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI Reasoning for Wireless Communications and Networking: A Survey and Perspectives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:09:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:19:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) techniques play a pivotal role in optimizing wireless communication networks. However, traditional deep learning approaches often act as closed boxes, lacking the structured reasoning abilities needed to tackle complex, multi-step decision problems. This survey provides a comprehensive review and outlook of reasoning-enabled AI in wireless communication networks, with a focus on Large Language Models (LLMs) and other advanced reasoning paradigms. In particular, LLM-based agents can combine reasoning with long-term planning, memory, tool utilization, and autonomous cross-layer control to dynamically optimize network operations with minimal human intervention. We begin by outlining the evolution of intelligent wireless networking and the limitations of conventional AI methods. We then introduce emerging AI reasoning techniques. Furthermore, we establish a classification system applicable to wireless network tasks. We also present a layer-by-layer examination for AI reasoning, covering the physical, data link, network, transport, and application layers. For each part, we identify key challenges and illustrate how AI reasoning methods can improve AI-based wireless communication performance. Finally, we discuss key research directions for AI reasoning toward future wireless communication networks. By combining insights from both communications and AI, this survey aims to chart a path for integrating reasoning techniques into the next-generation wireless networks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09193","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:08Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:28:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09154","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09154","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09154","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bui Duc","familyName":"Manh","name":"Manh, Bui Duc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soumyaratna","familyName":"Debnath","name":"Debnath, Soumyaratna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zetong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zetong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shriram","familyName":"Damodaran","name":"Damodaran, Shriram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arvind","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Arvind","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yueyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Mi","name":"Mi, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Cambria","name":"Cambria, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mind Meets Space: Rethinking Agentic Spatial Intelligence from a Neuroscience-inspired Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T05:23:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in agentic AI have led to systems capable of autonomous task execution and language-based reasoning, yet their spatial reasoning abilities remain limited and underexplored, largely constrained to symbolic and sequential processing. In contrast, human spatial intelligence, rooted in integrated multisensory perception, spatial memory, and cognitive maps, enables flexible, context-aware decision-making in unstructured environments. Therefore, bridging this gap is critical for advancing Agentic Spatial Intelligence toward better interaction with the physical 3D world. To this end, we first start from scrutinizing the spatial neural models as studied in computational neuroscience, and accordingly introduce a novel computational framework grounded in neuroscience principles. This framework maps core biological functions to six essential computation modules: bio-inspired multimodal sensing, multi-sensory integration, egocentric-allocentric conversion, an artificial cognitive map, spatial memory, and spatial reasoning. Together, these modules form a perspective landscape for agentic spatial reasoning capability across both virtual and physical environments. On top, we conduct a framework-guided analysis of recent methods, evaluating their relevance to each module and identifying critical gaps that hinder the development of more neuroscience-grounded spatial reasoning modules. We further examine emerging benchmarks and datasets and explore potential application domains ranging from virtual to embodied systems, such as robotics. Finally, we outline potential research directions, emphasizing the promising roadmap that can generalize spatial reasoning across dynamic or unstructured environments. We hope this work will benefit the research community with a neuroscience-grounded perspective and a structured pathway. Our project page can be found at Github."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"54 pages, journal"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09154","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:11Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:27:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09131","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09131","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09131","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phuong-Nam","familyName":"Dang","name":"Dang, Phuong-Nam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kieu-Linh","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Kieu-Linh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh-Hieu","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Thanh-Hieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ViRanker: A BGE-M3 \u0026amp; Blockwise Parallel Transformer Cross-Encoder for Vietnamese Reranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T04:07:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:14:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents ViRanker, a cross-encoder reranking model tailored to the Vietnamese language. Built on the BGE-M3 encoder and enhanced with the Blockwise Parallel Transformer, ViRanker addresses the lack of competitive rerankers for Vietnamese, a low-resource language with complex syntax and diacritics. The model was trained on an 8 GB curated corpus and fine-tuned with hybrid hard-negative sampling to strengthen robustness. Evaluated on the MMARCO-VI benchmark, ViRanker achieves strong early-rank accuracy, surpassing multilingual baselines and competing closely with PhoRanker. By releasing the model openly on Hugging Face, we aim to support reproducibility and encourage wider adoption in real-world retrieval systems. Beyond Vietnamese, this study illustrates how careful architectural adaptation and data curation can advance reranking in other underrepresented languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09131","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:26:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09103","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09103","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09103","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanti Raju","familyName":"Mylay","name":"Mylay, Chanti Raju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bobin","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Bobin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Honghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T02:29:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:12:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Crop diseases pose significant threats to global food security, agricultural productivity, and sustainable farming practices, directly affecting farmers' livelihoods and economic stability. To address the growing need for effective crop disease management, AI-based disease alerting systems have emerged as promising tools by providing early detection and actionable insights for timely intervention. However, existing systems often overlook critical aspects such as data privacy, market pricing power, and farmer-friendly usability, leaving farmers vulnerable to privacy breaches and economic exploitation. To bridge these gaps, we propose AgriSentinel, the first Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System. AgriSentinel incorporates a differential privacy mechanism to protect sensitive crop image data while maintaining classification accuracy. Its lightweight deep learning-based crop disease classification model is optimized for mobile devices, ensuring accessibility and usability for farmers. Additionally, the system includes a fine-tuned, on-device large language model (LLM) that leverages a curated knowledge pool to provide farmers with specific, actionable suggestions for managing crop diseases, going beyond simple alerting. Comprehensive experiments validate the effectiveness of AgriSentinel, demonstrating its ability to safeguard data privacy, maintain high classification performance, and deliver practical, actionable disease management strategies. AgriSentinel offers a robust, farmer-friendly solution for automating crop disease alerting and management, ultimately contributing to improved agricultural decision-making and enhanced crop productivity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09103","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:57Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09090","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09090","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09090","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyu","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Hengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yijiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanrui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Huanrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SQAP-VLA: A Synergistic Quantization-Aware Pruning Framework for High-Performance Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T01:52:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:11:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models exhibit unprecedented capabilities for embodied intelligence. However, their extensive computational and memory costs hinder their practical deployment. Existing VLA compression and acceleration approaches conduct quantization or token pruning in an ad-hoc manner but fail to enable both for a holistic efficiency improvement due to an observed incompatibility. This work introduces SQAP-VLA, the first structured, training-free VLA inference acceleration framework that simultaneously enables state-of-the-art quantization and token pruning. We overcome the incompatibility by co-designing the quantization and token pruning pipeline, where we propose new quantization-aware token pruning criteria that work on an aggressively quantized model while improving the quantizer design to enhance pruning effectiveness. When applied to standard VLA models, SQAP-VLA yields significant gains in computational efficiency and inference speed while successfully preserving core model performance, achieving a $\\times$1.93 speedup and up to a 4.5\\% average success rate enhancement compared to the original model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09090","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:38Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09055","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09055","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09055","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piyush","familyName":"Pant","name":"Pant, Piyush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving LLM Safety and Helpfulness using SFT and DPO: A Study on OPT-350M"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T23:22:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:08:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This research investigates the effectiveness of alignment techniques, Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), and a combined SFT+DPO approach on improving the safety and helpfulness of the OPT-350M language model. Utilizing the Anthropic Helpful-Harmless RLHF dataset, we train and evaluate four models: the base OPT350M, an SFT model, a DPO model, and a model trained with both SFT and DPO. We introduce three key evaluation metrics: Harmlessness Rate (HmR), Helpfulness Rate (HpR), and a Combined Alignment Score (CAS), all derived from reward model outputs. The results show that while SFT outperforms DPO, The combined SFT+DPO model outperforms all others across all metrics, demonstrating the complementary nature of these techniques. Our findings also highlight challenges posed by noisy data, limited GPU resources, and training constraints. This study offers a comprehensive view of how fine-tuning strategies affect model alignment and provides a foundation for more robust alignment pipelines in future work."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 3 figures. Code and dataset available at https://github.com/PiyushWithPant/Improving-LLM-Safety-and-Helpfulness-using-SFT-and-DPO"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09055","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:24:49Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:24:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:24:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diana Vieira","familyName":"Fernandes","name":"Fernandes, Diana Vieira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soummya","familyName":"Kar","name":"Kar, Soummya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos Santos","familyName":"Silva","name":"Silva, Carlos Santos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decentralized Local Voltage Control for Active Distribution Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"49","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T22:55:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:08:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Distribution networks face challenges from the increasing deployment of Distributed Energy Resources (DERs) and the emergence of bidirectional power flows. We propose a decentralized Volt/VAr control method based on a saddle-point reformulation and consensus+innovation (C+I) updates. Each agent at a controllable bus computes and enforces its own set-points using only neighbor communication. Our method embeds passive buses directly, preserves network physics through a linearized Jacobian model, and avoids any supervisory nodes. Simulation results on a modified CIGRE low-voltage network show voltage stability improvement within operational limits, indicating the viability of a fully decentralized (edge-based) Volt/VAr control solution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in IEEE SmartGridComm'25 - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Communications, Control, and Computing Technologies for Smart Grids (SmartGridComm)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:24:40Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:24:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:24:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09015","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09015","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09015","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenqian","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Chenqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikasadat","familyName":"Emami","name":"Emami, Nikasadat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kushagra","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Kushagra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xujin \"Chris\"","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xujin \"Chris\"","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xupeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VoxelFormer: Parameter-Efficient Multi-Subject Visual Decoding from fMRI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T21:20:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:06:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in fMRI-based visual decoding have enabled compelling reconstructions of perceived images. However, most approaches rely on subject-specific training, limiting scalability and practical deployment. We introduce \\textbf{VoxelFormer}, a lightweight transformer architecture that enables multi-subject training for visual decoding from fMRI. VoxelFormer integrates a Token Merging Transformer (ToMer) for efficient voxel compression and a query-driven Q-Former that produces fixed-size neural representations aligned with the CLIP image embedding space. Evaluated on the 7T Natural Scenes Dataset, VoxelFormer achieves competitive retrieval performance on subjects included during training with significantly fewer parameters than existing methods. These results highlight token merging and query-based transformers as promising strategies for parameter-efficient neural decoding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09015","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:23:54Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:23:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:23:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08997","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08997","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08997","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaman","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yaman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiren","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yiren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacky","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jacky","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"YouthSafe: A Youth-Centric Safety Benchmark and Safeguard Model for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T20:47:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:05:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used by teenagers and young adults in everyday life, ranging from emotional support and creative expression to educational assistance. However, their unique vulnerabilities and risk profiles remain under-examined in current safety benchmarks and moderation systems, leaving this population disproportionately exposed to harm. In this work, we present Youth AI Risk (YAIR), the first benchmark dataset designed to evaluate and improve the safety of youth LLM interactions. YAIR consists of 12,449 annotated conversation snippets spanning 78 fine grained risk types, grounded in a taxonomy of youth specific harms such as grooming, boundary violation, identity confusion, and emotional overreliance. We systematically evaluate widely adopted moderation models on YAIR and find that existing approaches substantially underperform in detecting youth centered risks, often missing contextually subtle yet developmentally harmful interactions. To address these gaps, we introduce YouthSafe, a real-time risk detection model optimized for youth GenAI contexts. YouthSafe significantly outperforms prior systems across multiple metrics on risk detection and classification, offering a concrete step toward safer and more developmentally appropriate AI interactions for young users."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08997","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:23:29Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:23:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:23:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08960","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08960","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08960","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thales Sales","familyName":"Almeida","name":"Almeida, Thales Sales","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovana Kerche","familyName":"Bonás","name":"Bonás, Giovana Kerche","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"João Guilherme Alves","familyName":"Santos","name":"Santos, João Guilherme Alves","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BRoverbs -- Measuring how much LLMs understand Portuguese proverbs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T19:47:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:04:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) exhibit significant performance variations depending on the linguistic and cultural context in which they are applied. This disparity signals the necessity of mature evaluation frameworks that can assess their capabilities in specific regional settings. In the case of Portuguese, existing evaluations remain limited, often relying on translated datasets that may not fully capture linguistic nuances or cultural references. Meanwhile, native Portuguese-language datasets predominantly focus on structured national exams or sentiment analysis of social media interactions, leaving gaps in evaluating broader linguistic understanding. To address this limitation, we introduce BRoverbs, a dataset specifically designed to assess LLM performance through Brazilian proverbs. Proverbs serve as a rich linguistic resource, encapsulating cultural wisdom, figurative expressions, and complex syntactic structures that challenge the model comprehension of regional expressions. BRoverbs aims to provide a new evaluation tool for Portuguese-language LLMs, contributing to advancing regionally informed benchmarking. The benchmark is available at https://huggingface.co/datasets/Tropic-AI/BRoverbs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08960","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:22:37Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:22:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:22:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08956","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08956","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08956","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Snow","name":"Snow, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikram","familyName":"Krishnamurthy","name":"Krishnamurthy, Vikram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Inverse Reinforcement Learning for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination -- A Distributionally Robust Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T19:30:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:04:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent inverse reinforcement learning (IRL) aims to identify Pareto-efficient behavior in a multi-agent system, and reconstruct utility functions of the individual agents. Motivated by the problem of detecting UAV coordination, how can we construct a statistical detector for Pareto-efficient behavior given noisy measurements of the decisions of a multi-agent system? This paper approaches this IRL problem by deriving necessary and sufficient conditions for a dataset of multi-agent system dynamics to be consistent with Pareto-efficient coordination, and providing algorithms for recovering utility functions which are consistent with the system dynamics. We derive an optimal statistical detector for determining Pareto-efficient coordination from noisy system measurements, which minimizes Type-I statistical detection error. Then, we provide a utility estimation algorithm which minimizes the worst-case estimation error over a statistical ambiguity set centered at empirical observations; this min-max solution achieves distributionally robust IRL, which is crucial in adversarial strategic interactions. We illustrate these results in a detailed example for detecting Pareto-efficient coordination among multiple UAVs given noisy measurement recorded at a radar. We then reconstruct the utility functions of the UAVs in a distributionally robust sense."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08956","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:22:31Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:22:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:22:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08950","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08950","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08950","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jarvis","familyName":"Haupt","name":"Haupt, Jarvis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanning","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yanning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"McCreary","name":"McCreary, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehmet","familyName":"Akçakaya","name":"Akçakaya, Mehmet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios B.","familyName":"Giannakis","name":"Giannakis, Georgios B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deploying AI for Signal Processing education: Selected challenges and intriguing opportunities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T19:19:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:04:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/msp.2025.3600846","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Powerful artificial intelligence (AI) tools that have emerged in recent years -- including large language models, automated coding assistants, and advanced image and speech generation technologies -- are the result of monumental human achievements. These breakthroughs reflect mastery across multiple technical disciplines and the resolution of significant technological challenges. However, some of the most profound challenges may still lie ahead. These challenges are not purely technical but pertain to the fair and responsible use of AI in ways that genuinely improve the global human condition. This article explores one promising application aligned with that vision: the use of AI tools to facilitate and enhance education, with a specific focus on signal processing (SP). It presents two interrelated perspectives: identifying and addressing technical limitations, and applying AI tools in practice to improve educational experiences. Primers are provided on several core technical issues that arise when using AI in educational settings, including how to ensure fairness and inclusivity, handle hallucinated outputs, and achieve efficient use of resources. These and other considerations -- such as transparency, explainability, and trustworthiness -- are illustrated through the development of an immersive, structured, and reliable \"smart textbook.\" The article serves as a resource for researchers and educators seeking to advance AI's role in engineering education."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the IEEE Signal Processing Magazine Special Issue on Artificial Intelligence for Education: A Signal Processing Perspective"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08950","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-12T01:22:23Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:22:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:22:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08927","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08927","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08927","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lynnette Hui Xian","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Lynnette Hui Xian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bianca N. Y.","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Bianca N. Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathleen M.","familyName":"Carley","name":"Carley, Kathleen M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AuraSight: Generating Realistic Social Media Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:44:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:02:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This document details the narrative and technical design behind the process of generating a quasi-realistic set X data for a fictional multi-day pop culture episode (AuraSight). Social media post simulation is essential towards creating realistic training scenarios for understanding emergent network behavior that formed from known sets of agents. Our social media post generation pipeline uses the AESOP-SynSM engine, which employs a hybrid approach of agent-based and generative artificial intelligence techniques. We explicate choices in scenario setup and summarize the fictional groups involved, before moving on to the operationalization of these actors and their interactions within the SynSM engine. We also briefly illustrate some outputs generated and discuss the utility of such simulated data and potential future improvements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Carnegie Mellon University Technical Report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08927","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:21:51Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:21:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:21:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08910","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08910","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08910","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tung","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lam","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Lam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quynh","familyName":"Dao","name":"Dao, Quynh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PromptGuard: An Orchestrated Prompting Framework for Principled Synthetic Text Generation for Vulnerable Populations using LLMs with Enhanced Safety, Fairness, and Controllability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:01:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of Large Language Models (LLMs) in real-world applications poses unprecedented risks of generating harmful, biased, or misleading information to vulnerable populations including LGBTQ+ individuals, single parents, and marginalized communities. While existing safety approaches rely on post-hoc filtering or generic alignment techniques, they fail to proactively prevent harmful outputs at the generation source. This paper introduces PromptGuard, a novel modular prompting framework with our breakthrough contribution: VulnGuard Prompt, a hybrid technique that prevents harmful information generation using real-world data-driven contrastive learning. VulnGuard integrates few-shot examples from curated GitHub repositories, ethical chain-of-thought reasoning, and adaptive role-prompting to create population-specific protective barriers. Our framework employs theoretical multi-objective optimization with formal proofs demonstrating 25-30% analytical harm reduction through entropy bounds and Pareto optimality. PromptGuard orchestrates six core modules: Input Classification, VulnGuard Prompting, Ethical Principles Integration, External Tool Interaction, Output Validation, and User-System Interaction, creating an intelligent expert system for real-time harm prevention. We provide comprehensive mathematical formalization including convergence proofs, vulnerability analysis using information theory, and theoretical validation framework using GitHub-sourced datasets, establishing mathematical foundations for systematic empirical research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08910","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:21:28Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:21:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:21:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08890","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08890","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08890","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanda","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Wanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel J.","familyName":"Garratt","name":"Garratt, Samuel J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Norhan M.","familyName":"Eassa","name":"Eassa, Norhan M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eliott","familyName":"Rosenberg","name":"Rosenberg, Eliott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedram","familyName":"Roushan","name":"Roushan, Pedram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Zhuang","familyName":"You","name":"You, Yi-Zhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehud","familyName":"Altman","name":"Altman, Ehud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Machine learning the effects of many quantum measurements"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:00:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:01:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Measurements are essential for the processing and protection of information in quantum computers. They can also induce long-range entanglement between unmeasured qubits. However, when post-measurement states depend on many non-deterministic measurement outcomes, there is a barrier to observing and using the entanglement induced by prior measurements. Here we demonstrate a new approach for detecting such measurement-induced entanglement. We create short-range entangled states of one- and two-dimensional arrays of qubits in a superconducting quantum processor, and aim to characterize the long-range entanglement induced between distant pairs of qubits when we measure all of the others. To do this we use unsupervised training of neural networks on observations to create computational models for post-measurement states and, by correlating these models with experimental data, we reveal measurement-induced entanglement. Our results additionally demonstrate a transition in the ability of a classical agent to accurately model the experimental data; this is closely related to a measurement-induced phase transition. We anticipate that our work can act as a basis for future experiments on quantum error correction and more general problems in quantum control."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08890","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:21:00Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:21:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:21:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08865","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08865","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08865","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Guangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xixuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiyan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Peiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhai","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Yanhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TraceRAG: A LLM-Based Framework for Explainable Android Malware Detection and Behavior Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T06:07:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sophisticated evasion tactics in malicious Android applications, combined with their intricate behavioral semantics, enable attackers to conceal malicious logic within legitimate functions, underscoring the critical need for robust and in-depth analysis frameworks. However, traditional analysis techniques often fail to recover deeply hidden behaviors or provide human-readable justifications for their decisions. Inspired by advances in large language models (LLMs), we introduce TraceRAG, a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) framework that bridges natural language queries and Java code to deliver explainable malware detection and analysis. First, TraceRAG generates summaries of method-level code snippets, which are indexed in a vector database. At query time, behavior-focused questions retrieve the most semantically relevant snippets for deeper inspection. Finally, based on the multi-turn analysis results, TraceRAG produces human-readable reports that present the identified malicious behaviors and their corresponding code implementations. Experimental results demonstrate that our method achieves 96\\% malware detection accuracy and 83.81\\% behavior identification accuracy based on updated VirusTotal (VT) scans and manual verification. Furthermore, expert evaluation confirms the practical utility of the reports generated by TraceRAG."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08865","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:20:25Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:20:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:20:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08859","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08859","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08859","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"Suriani","name":"Suriani, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"Affinita","name":"Affinita, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Domenico D.","familyName":"Bloisi","name":"Bloisi, Domenico D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"Nardi","name":"Nardi, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi Robot Coordination in Highly Dynamic Environments: Tackling Asymmetric Obstacles and Limited Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T22:11:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coordinating a fully distributed multi-agent system (MAS) can be challenging when the communication channel has very limited capabilities in terms of sending rate and packet payload. When the MAS has to deal with active obstacles in a highly partially observable environment, the communication channel acquires considerable relevance. In this paper, we present an approach to deal with task assignments in extremely active scenarios, where tasks need to be frequently reallocated among the agents participating in the coordination process. Inspired by market-based task assignments, we introduce a novel distributed coordination method to orchestrate autonomous agents' actions efficiently in low communication scenarios. In particular, our algorithm takes into account asymmetric obstacles. While in the real world, the majority of obstacles are asymmetric, they are usually treated as symmetric ones, thus limiting the applicability of existing methods. To summarize, the presented architecture is designed to tackle scenarios where the obstacles are active and asymmetric, the communication channel is poor and the environment is partially observable. Our approach has been validated in simulation and in the real world, using a team of NAO robots during official RoboCup competitions. Experimental results show a notable reduction in task overlaps in limited communication settings, with a decrease of 52% in the most frequent reallocated task."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The 19th International Conference on Intelligent Autonomous Systems (IAS 19), 2025, Genoa"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08859","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:20:16Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:20:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:20:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08857","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08857","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08857","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcelino","familyName":"Garcia","name":"Garcia, Marcelino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renato","familyName":"Garcia","name":"Garcia, Renato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Parizotto","name":"Parizotto, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andre","familyName":"Mendes","name":"Mendes, Andre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Valle","name":"Valle, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo","familyName":"Vilela","name":"Vilela, Ricardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renato","familyName":"Balancieri","name":"Balancieri, Renato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Williamson","familyName":"Silva","name":"Silva, Williamson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Systematic Mapping Study on Chatbots in Programming Education"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T15:25:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Educational chatbots have gained prominence as support tools for teaching programming, particularly in introductory learning contexts. This paper presents a Systematic Mapping Study (SMS) that investigated how such agents have been developed and applied in programming education. From an initial set of 3,216 publications, 54 studies were selected and analyzed based on five research subquestions, addressing chatbot types, programming languages used, educational content covered, interaction models, and application contexts. The results reveal a predominance of chatbots designed for Python instruction, focusing on fundamental programming concepts, and employing a wide variety of pedagogical approaches and technological architectures. In addition to identifying trends and gaps in the literature, this study provides insights to inform the development of new educational tools for programming instruction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 1 figure, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08857","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:20:13Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:20:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:20:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08847","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08847","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08847","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amna","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Amna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Unity Game Template Generation from GDDs via NLP and Multi-Modal LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T21:53:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a novel framework for automated game template generation by transforming Game Design Documents (GDDs) into functional Unity game prototypes using Natural Language Processing (NLP) and multi-modal Large Language Models (LLMs). We introduce an end-to-end system that parses GDDs, extracts structured game specifications, and synthesizes Unity-compatible C# code that implements the core mechanics, systems, and architecture defined in the design documentation. Our approach combines a fine-tuned LLaMA-3 model specialized for Unity code generation with a custom Unity integration package that streamlines the implementation process. Evaluation results demonstrate significant improvements over baseline models, with our fine-tuned model achieving superior performance (4.8/5.0 average score) compared to state-of-the-art LLMs across compilation success, GDD adherence, best practices adoption, and code modularity metrics. The generated templates demonstrate high adherence to GDD specifications across multiple game genres. Our system effectively addresses critical gaps in AI-assisted game development, positioning LLMs as valuable tools in streamlining the transition from game design to implementation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08847","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:19:58Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:19:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:19:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08839","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08839","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08839","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aarav","familyName":"Mann","name":"Mann, Aarav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Vaz","name":"Vaz, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin E.","familyName":"Caldwell","name":"Caldwell, Benjamin E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Scholz","name":"Scholz, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Awad","name":"Awad, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rocky","familyName":"Allemandi","name":"Allemandi, Rocky","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doug","familyName":"Faust","name":"Faust, Doug","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshita","familyName":"Banka","name":"Banka, Harshita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Rousmaniere","name":"Rousmaniere, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating the Clinical Safety of LLMs in Response to High-Risk Mental Health Disclosures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-01T16:01:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) increasingly mediate emotionally sensitive conversations, especially in mental health contexts, their ability to recognize and respond to high-risk situations becomes a matter of public safety. This study evaluates the responses of six popular LLMs (Claude, Gemini, Deepseek, ChatGPT, Grok 3, and LLAMA) to user prompts simulating crisis-level mental health disclosures. Drawing on a coding framework developed by licensed clinicians, five safety-oriented behaviors were assessed: explicit risk acknowledgment, empathy, encouragement to seek help, provision of specific resources, and invitation to continue the conversation. Claude outperformed all others in global assessment, while Grok 3, ChatGPT, and LLAMA underperformed across multiple domains. Notably, most models exhibited empathy, but few consistently provided practical support or sustained engagement. These findings suggest that while LLMs show potential for emotionally attuned communication, none currently meet satisfactory clinical standards for crisis response. Ongoing development and targeted fine-tuning are essential to ensure ethical deployment of AI in mental health settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Previously posted as a preprint on Research Square (DOI: 10.21203/rs.3.rs-7364128/v1), under a CC BY 4.0 License"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08839","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:19:48Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:19:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:19:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08835","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08835","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08835","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent C.","familyName":"Müller","name":"Müller, Vincent C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deep opacity and AI: A threat to XAI and to privacy protection mechanisms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-30T11:15:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It is known that big data analytics and AI pose a threat to privacy, and that some of this is due to some kind of \"black box problem\" in AI. I explain how this becomes a problem in the context of justification for judgments and actions. Furthermore, I suggest distinguishing three kinds of opacity: 1) the subjects do not know what the system does (\"shallow opacity\"), 2) the analysts do not know what the system does (\"standard black box opacity\"), or 3) the analysts cannot possibly know what the system might do (\"deep opacity\"). If the agents, data subjects as well as analytics experts, operate under opacity, then these agents cannot provide justifications for judgments that are necessary to protect privacy, e.g., they cannot give \"informed consent\", or guarantee \"anonymity\". It follows from these points that agents in big data analytics and AI often cannot make the judgments needed to protect privacy. So I conclude that big data analytics makes the privacy problems worse and the remedies less effective. As a positive note, I provide a brief outlook on technical ways to handle this situation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08835","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:19:42Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:19:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:19:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":83,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+LM+OR+LMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+MoE+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+agentic%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+LM+OR+LMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+MoE+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+agentic%29%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28agent+OR+agents+OR+agentic+OR+retrieval+OR+memory+OR+memories+OR+safety+OR+jailbreak+OR+jailbreaks+OR+planning+OR+tool+OR+tools+OR+tool-calling%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28agent+OR+agents+OR+agentic+OR+retrieval+OR+memory+OR+memories+OR+safety+OR+jailbreak+OR+jailbreaks+OR+planning+OR+tool+OR+tools+OR+tool-calling%29%29+AND+registered%3A%5B2025-09-11T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-12T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026sort=registered"}}